module.exports={"principles":"PRINCIPLES (Ashtanga Hridaya, Sutrasthana). Health = balance of three doshas + strong even digestion (Agni) + healthy tissues + clear elimination. Like increases like; opposite reduces. VATA (air/space: dry,light,cold,rough,mobile) -> calmed by warm, oily, nourishing, grounding, steady routine. PITTA (fire/water: hot,sharp,oily,intense) -> calmed by cool, fresh, calm, moderate. KAPHA (earth/water: heavy,cold,slow,oily,stable) -> balanced by light, warm, dry, active, stimulating. Dinacharya (daily routine): wake at/before sunrise and don't resleep; elimination; warm water; oil self-massage; exercise to about half capacity; bathe; MAIN meal at midday when Agni is strongest; regular meal times; light early dinner; calm early sleep. Eat only when the previous meal is digested; irregular, late or excess food weakens Agni and creates 'ama' (toxins) that underlie most disease. Six tastes (Rasa): sweet/sour/salty reduce Vata and raise Kapha; bitter/pungent/astringent reduce Kapha and raise Vata; sweet/bitter/astringent reduce Pitta while sour/salty/pungent raise it. Ritucharya: adjust food and habits to the season. Ojas (vitality) is depleted by anger, worry, grief, starvation and overwork, and restored by nourishing food, calm and rest.","remedies":[{"problem":"The three doshas (Vata, Pitta, Kapha) and how they keep you well","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","guidance":"Ayurveda sees the body as run by three working forces. Vata governs all movement (breath, circulation, nerve signals, elimination) and is tied to air and space. Pitta governs transformation, heat and digestion and is tied to fire. Kapha governs structure, moisture and steadiness and is tied to water and earth. Health is simply these three staying in balance; illness is any one becoming too high or too low. They sit throughout the body but each has a base: Kapha from the chest up, Pitta between chest and navel, Vata below the navel. Understanding which force is disturbed is the first step before choosing food, routine or any remedy, because ev","chapter":1},{"problem":"Qualities of Vata and how to settle it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Vata carries six qualities: dry (ruksha), light (laghu), cold (sheeta), rough (khara), subtle or able to slip into small spaces (sookshma), and mobile (chala). Because like increases like, dry cold windy conditions, raw and dry foods, and constant movement all push Vata up and can show as dry skin, cracking joints, gas, anxiety and broken sleep. The way back is the opposite set of qualities: warmth, oiliness, steadiness and routine. Warm cooked moist food, oil massage, rest and a regular daily rhythm all counter Vata's dry, cold, moving nature.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Qualities of Pitta and how to settle it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Pitta carries these qualities: slightly oily (sasneha), sharp and penetrating (teekshna), hot (ushna), light (laghu), foul-smelling (visra), flowing (sara), and liquid (drava). Heat, sharpness and sourness raise it, often showing as acidity, burning, skin flare-ups, loose heat in the gut and a short temper. Since opposites calm a dosha, cooling, calming and slightly drying measures help: cooling foods, sweet-bitter-astringent tastes, avoiding excess sour, salty and pungent food, and staying out of fierce heat. The aim is to take the edge off Pitta's hot, sharp, liquid nature.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Qualities of Kapha and how to settle it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Kapha carries these qualities: oily (snigdha), cold (sheeta), heavy (guru), slow and dense (manda), smooth (shlakshna), slimy or sticky (mrutsna), and stable or unmoving (sthira). Heavy, cold, oily and sweet things increase it, showing as sluggish digestion, mucus and congestion, weight gain and a dull, attached feeling. The opposite qualities lighten it: warmth, dryness, stimulation and movement. Lighter warm food, the pungent-bitter-astringent tastes, exercise and keeping active all help bring down Kapha's heavy, cold, stable nature.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The seven body tissues (Dhatus)","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"From digested food the body builds seven tissues in order, each feeding the next: Rasa (the first nourishing fluid, broadly like plasma and lymph), Rakta (blood), Mamsa (muscle), Meda (fat), Asthi (bone and cartilage), Majja (marrow), and Shukra (reproductive tissue and its secretions in both men and women). Together with the wastes they are called Dushyas, meaning the things the doshas act on and spoil in disease. Strong, well-formed tissues depend on good digestion, because each tissue is made from the one before it. This explains the Ayurvedic focus on digestion as the root of overall nourishment and strength.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The three wastes (Malas) and why they matter","category":"Detox","dosha":"—","guidance":"The body makes three main wastes: Pureesha or Shakrut (faeces), Mootra (urine), and Sweda (sweat). Though called wastes, they each do a job while present, and their regular, easy passage is a sign of health. Problems with any of them point to disturbed doshas or weak digestion. This is the basis for the later teaching that natural urges should never be forced or held back: smooth elimination keeps the inner system clean and balanced.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The six tastes (Rasa) and their effect on the body","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","guidance":"Food acts through six tastes: sweet (Madhura), sour (Amla), salty (Lavana), bitter (Tikta), pungent (Katu) and astringent (Kashaya). In that order they give progressively less nourishing energy, so sweet is the most strengthening and astringent the least. Each taste also moves the doshas. Sweet, sour and salty reduce Vata but build Kapha. Bitter, pungent and astringent reduce Kapha but raise Vata. Astringent, bitter and sweet reduce Pitta, while sour, salty and pungent raise it. A balanced meal uses the tastes to steady whichever dosha is prone to rise, rather than piling on tastes that worsen it.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Potency (Veerya) and post-digestive effect (Vipaka) of foods","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"—","guidance":"Beyond taste, every food has a potency, either heating (Ushna Veerya, like pepper) or cooling (Sheeta Veerya, like milk). As a rule the astringent, bitter and sweet tastes tend to cool, while sour, salty and pungent tend to heat. After the first stage of digestion the taste changes into a final effect called Vipaka, of three kinds: sweet Vipaka (usually from sweet and salty foods), sour Vipaka (from sour foods), and pungent Vipaka (from bitter, pungent and astringent foods). Choosing foods by their heating or cooling nature, not taste alone, is how Ayurveda matches diet to a person's current state and the season.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The digestive fire (Agni) and its four types","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","guidance":"Digestion is run by Agni, and its pattern follows the doshas. Vishama Agni (Vata-driven) is irregular, with appetite that swings between strong and weak. Teekshna Agni (Pitta-driven) is sharp, with a fierce appetite and very fast digestion that can leave you hungry again quickly. Manda Agni (Kapha-driven) is dull, with weak appetite and slow, heavy digestion. Sama Agni (from balanced doshas) is the ideal: steady appetite with food digesting fully at the proper time. Knowing your Agni type guides how much and how often to eat, since the goal is to steady digestion toward the balanced Sama state.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Knowing your body type (Prakruti)","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","guidance":"Each person has a fixed constitution set at conception, much as poison is natural to a poisonous creature. Some people run more Vata, some more Pitta, some more Kapha, some are balanced across all three, and some are two-dosha mixes. The text traditionally rates a balanced tridosha type as the most resilient, Kapha-dominant as good, Pitta-dominant as middling and Vata-dominant as more delicate, with two-dosha types seen as less steady. This is about natural tendencies and resilience, not a person's value. Knowing your type helps you anticipate which dosha is most likely to rise in you and adjust food and routine before trouble starts.","chapter":1},{"problem":"The rule of like increases like, opposites reduce","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","guidance":"The core law of this whole system is that similar qualities increase a thing and opposite qualities reduce it. Dryness is a Vata quality, so dry cold weather raises Vata and dries the skin, while oil, being the opposite of dryness, brings it back down. The twenty qualities of matter come in ten opposite pairs (heavy/light, slow/sharp, cold/hot, oily/dry, smooth/rough, solid/liquid, soft/hard, stable/moving, subtle/gross, non-slimy/slimy). Whenever something is disturbed, you apply its opposite quality. This one rule lets you reason about any food, activity or climate: ask which qualities it adds, and whether those are what you need more of or","chapter":1},{"problem":"What makes you healthy or sick (time, senses, action)","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"Three factors decide health: time (kala), contact between the senses and their objects (artha), and our actions (karma). When our use of these is balanced and appropriate, we stay well. When it is too little, too much, or plain wrong, disease follows. For example, too much sun, too little sleep, or misusing the senses all tip the balance. In short, health is dosha balance and disease is dosha imbalance. The practical lesson is that most illness traces back to how we handle timing, our senses and our daily conduct, which is exactly why Ayurveda puts such weight on daily and seasonal routine.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Caring for the mind (mental doshas and their remedy)","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"The mind has its own two doshas. Rajas drives activity and, in excess, agitation and restlessness. Tamas brings dullness, inaction and lethargy. Just as the body falls ill from imbalance, so does the mind. The text's remedy for mental imbalance is threefold: Dhee (sharpening understanding and discernment), Dhairya (building courage, patience and steadiness), and Atmavijnana (self-knowledge or self-realisation). This is educational, traditional guidance for cultivating a steady mind, and sits alongside, not in place of, modern mental-health care.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Two kinds of treatment: Shamana and Shodhana","category":"Detox","dosha":"Vata, Pitta, Kapha","guidance":"Treatment comes in two broad forms. Shamana (pacifying) gently brings a disturbed dosha back to balance and is suited to early stages. Shodhana (purification) actively expels the excess dosha from the body and is used when the imbalance is advanced. The classic pairings are: for Vata, Basti (medicated enema) purifies while oil pacifies; for Pitta, Virechana (purgation) purifies while ghee pacifies; for Kapha, Vamana (therapeutic vomiting) purifies while honey pacifies. These purification therapies are serious clinical procedures meant to be done under a trained practitioner, not at home. Educational context only.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Signs of good health and the four aims of life","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"Ayurveda treats a long, healthy life as the foundation that lets a person pursue the four real aims of living: Dharma (living rightly), Artha (earning fairly), Kama (meeting legitimate desires), and Moksha (spiritual freedom). Good health shows up as balanced doshas, steady digestion (Sama Agni), easy regular elimination of the three wastes, well-nourished tissues, restful sleep and a calm, clear mind. Because none of life's aims can be pursued when you are unwell, the text urges putting steady effort into protecting health, and names humility and willingness to learn (Vidheya) as the first quality of anyone who wants to live well.","chapter":1},{"problem":"Waking time and the start of the morning routine","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","guidance":"A healthy person should rise during Brahma Muhurta, the last part of the night before dawn, roughly between 3 am and 6 am and ideally about 45 minutes before sunrise. This quiet, fresh hour is traditionally said to protect life and health and to give the day a calm, clear start. The very first things on waking are to empty the bladder and bowels, and then to clean the teeth, before moving on to the rest of the routine. Building the day around an early, consistent waking time is the foundation that the whole daily regimen rests on.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Cleaning the teeth (Dantadhavana)","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"After relieving yourself, clean the teeth. The traditional method uses a fresh chewing-twig from an astringent, pungent or bitter plant such as Arka (Calotropis), banyan, Khadira (Acacia catechu), Karanja (Pongamia) or Arjuna. The twig should be about as thick as the tip of your little finger and about twelve finger-widths long, with one end chewed soft into bristles. Brush so that the teeth are cleaned without injuring the gums. The astringent-pungent-bitter taste is chosen deliberately because it helps clear the sticky Kapha and coating that build up in the mouth overnight. A modern soft brush achieves the same goal of gentle, thorough dail","chapter":2},{"problem":"When to skip vigorous tooth brushing","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","guidance":"Vigorous twig-brushing is to be avoided while you are in certain unwell states, because the strong action can aggravate them. These include indigestion, vomiting, breathlessness, cough, fever, facial paralysis, severe thirst, ulcers in the mouth, heart disease, and disorders of the eyes, head or ears. During such times gentle care is better than forceful brushing. The underlying lesson is to adjust even daily habits to the body's current condition rather than following them mechanically.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Eye care with collyrium (Anjana)","category":"Eyes/Head","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Traditionally a healthy person applies a special antimony-based collyrium called Sauveera Anjana to the eyes each day, because the eyes are seen as fiery in nature and therefore easily troubled by cold, heavy Kapha. Once a week a stronger preparation, Rasanjana (an aqueous extract of Berberis aristata), is used to help drain excess Kapha secretions from the eyes. This is described as part of the classical daily routine and is given here for educational understanding of how the text cares for the eyes; it is not a prescription for self-treatment.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Nasal drops, gargling, smoke and betel after eye care","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"After eye care the routine continues in order: Nasya, instilling drops into the nose using milk, herbal decoction or medicated oil; then Gandusha, holding or gargling warm water, milk, herbal decoction or oil in the mouth; then Dhuma, inhaling medicinal herbal smoke; and finally chewing betel leaf (Tambula). Together these care for the nose, mouth and throat and clear the heavy Kapha that accumulates overnight, leaving the head and senses feeling light and clear. These are classical practices; the everyday takeaway is the value of warm-water gargling and keeping the nasal passages clear each morning.","chapter":2},{"problem":"When to avoid betel leaf chewing (Tambula)","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","guidance":"Chewing betel leaf is part of the classical routine, but the text names clear exceptions where it should be avoided because it can worsen the condition: open wounds, bleeding disorders, excessive dryness of the body, redness of the eyes, poisoning, repeated fainting spells, intoxication, and tuberculosis. As with tooth-cleaning, the principle is to match the practice to the state of the body and leave it out when the body is already strained.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Daily oil massage (Abhyanga)","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Oil massage done every morning is one of the most valued daily practices. It slows ageing, relieves tiredness and eases the aches and pains that come from excess Vata. It also sharpens eyesight, nourishes the body tissues, lengthens life, brings sound sleep, and improves the tone and texture of the skin. Give special attention to three areas: the head, the ears and the feet. Important exceptions: avoid Abhyanga when Kapha is aggravated (for example during heavy congestion), immediately after a purification therapy like Panchakarma, and during indigestion. Warm oil suited to the season works best, applied unhurriedly before bathing.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Exercise (Vyayama) and how much is right","category":"Energy","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Exercise brings lightness, improves work capacity, strengthens digestion, burns fat and gives the body a firm, well-shaped form. The correct dose is to work only up to about half your capacity (ardha shakti), stopping when effort first begins to show rather than pushing to exhaustion. It is especially suited to the cold season and spring (roughly December to May), and should be kept mild in hotter times. It belongs mainly to those who are strong and eat rich, oily food. People with Vata or Pitta disorders, children, the elderly and anyone with indigestion should not do it. After exercising, the body should be gently massaged all over to settl","chapter":2},{"problem":"Harm from over-exercise and overexertion","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata, Pitta","guidance":"Pushing exercise too hard is actively harmful. It can cause excessive thirst, wasting of the body, severe breathlessness, bleeding disorders, deep exhaustion, a sense of weakness even without any work, cough, fever and vomiting. The text warns that a person who constantly over-exercises, stays up late night after night, walks very long distances, has excessive sex, or laughs and talks to excess, wears themselves out, like a lion that destroys itself even after defeating an elephant. The message is to respect the half-strength limit and to treat rest, sleep and restraint as part of staying strong, not the opposite of it.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Dry powder massage (Udvartana) for Kapha and fat","category":"Detox","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Udvartana is massage with dry herbal powders instead of oil. It calms aggravated Kapha and helps dissolve and burn fat, which is why Ayurvedic centres use it as part of anti-obesity care. It also firms and steadies the limbs and improves the skin's strength and complexion. Where oil massage is soothing and best for Vata dryness, powder massage is stimulating and drying, making it the better choice when the body feels heavy, congested or sluggish with Kapha. It is typically done before bathing.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Bathing (Snana) and the right water temperature","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","guidance":"Bathing strengthens digestion, acts as a tonic for vigour, lengthens life, and lifts enthusiasm and strength. It clears away dirt, sweat, waste, tiredness, excessive thirst, burning sensation and germs. One specific rule matters: warm water poured over the body gives strength, but hot water over the head harms the hair and the eyes, so wash the head with cooler water while the body can take warm. Bathing is best avoided by those with facial paralysis, diseases of the eyes, mouth or ears, diarrhoea, flatulence, a blocked or running nose, or indigestion, and by anyone who has just eaten.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Eating only after the last meal has digested","category":"Digestion","dosha":"—","guidance":"A simple but central rule of the daily routine: eat only after the previous meal has fully digested, and even then eat in a measured, limited amount (Jeerne hitam mitam cha adyat). Eating again before the last meal is digested burdens the digestive fire and breeds imbalance, while overeating overwhelms it. Waiting for genuine hunger, the sign that the earlier food has been processed, and then stopping before you are completely full, keeps digestion steady and the tissues well nourished. This single habit protects Agni, which the text treats as the root of overall health.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Do not suppress or force natural urges","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Two paired rules govern the body's natural urges. First, never force out an urge that is not there, such as straining to urinate or pass stool when there is no real need (Na vegan neerayet); forcing disturbs the downward Vata. Second, never hold back an urge that has genuinely arisen, attend to it promptly instead of staying busy with other tasks (Na vegito anya karyaha). Smooth, timely elimination keeps the inner system clean and the doshas, especially Vata, in order. The text adds a related point: seek treatment for any illness as early as possible rather than letting it settle in.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Good conduct of speech (right, kind, measured words)","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"The text treats speech as part of daily health. Speak at the right occasion (Kale bruyat), speak kind and pleasant words (Hitam bruyat), and speak sparingly, only as much as needed (Mitam bruyat). Avoid arguing and avoid untruth. Be the first to greet and open conversation, keep a pleasant face and good character, and be gentle and courteous in word and action. Do not broadcast insults you have suffered or your grievances against superiors. This disciplined, warm speech is listed among the practices that, over time, bring long life, good reputation and peace of mind.","chapter":2},{"problem":"The ten acts to give up (body, speech, mind)","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"The chapter names ten harmful acts to abandon, grouped by body, speech and mind. Of the body: violence or cruelty (Himsa), stealing (Steya), and wrongful sexual conduct (Anyatha-kama). Of speech: back-biting (Paishunya), harsh words (Parusha), lying (Anruta), and divisive talk that breaks friendships (Sambhinna-alapa). Of the mind: ill-will and the wish to harm (Vyapada), envy of others' good fortune (Abhidya), and perverse, faultfinding views and faithlessness (Drg-viparyaya). Giving these up is presented as mental and moral hygiene, the counterpart to physical hygiene, and part of what keeps a person well and free of inner turmoil.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Kindness, generosity and an even mind","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"The code of good conduct centres on compassion and balance. Help those who lack a livelihood, who are ill, or who are stricken with grief. Treat even the smallest creatures with respect, and honour physicians, elders and guests. Never turn away or abuse a beggar. Be helpful even to those who do not help you, even to foes. Above all, keep an even mind in both calamity and prosperity (Sampat-vipatsu eka-manaha), and do not envy others' wealth or happiness. The text sums up the essence of right conduct as compassion toward all beings, generosity, control of body, speech and mind, and treating others' interests as your own.","chapter":2},{"problem":"The middle path and care of the senses","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"A recurring instruction is to keep to the middle course in all things and avoid extremes. The senses should neither be strained and overworked nor pampered and indulged. Stop the activity of body, speech and mind before you reach the point of exhaustion, rather than pushing to depletion. Do not throw yourself into any undertaking empty of the aims of Dharma, Artha and Kama, and never act against Dharma. Deal with each person according to their nature, in the way most likely to please them. This balance, moderate effort, moderate restraint, is itself treated as preventive medicine for both body and mind.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Personal hygiene and grooming","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","guidance":"Keep yourself clean and well groomed. Trim the hair, nails and moustache regularly. Keep the feet, ears, nose, eyes, urethra and anus clean. Bathe every day, wear light pleasant scents, and dress in clean, neat clothes that are agreeable rather than flashy. When going out, carry an umbrella against sun and weather, wear footwear, and look ahead about four arm-lengths on your path. If urgent work takes you out at night, go equipped with a stick, a head-covering and a companion for safety. This everyday cleanliness and sensible precaution is part of the routine that protects health.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Places, postures and situations to avoid","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"The chapter lists many sensible cautions. Do not cross rivers by swimming on your arms, walk straight into a large fire, travel in an unsafe boat, climb a tree of doubtful strength, or ride a vehicle in bad condition. Do not sneeze, laugh or yawn without covering the mouth, nor blow the nose or scratch the ground needlessly, nor squat on the heels for long. Do not gaze at the sun for long or keep staring at tiny, dazzling, dirty or unpleasant objects. Avoid harsh draughts, fierce sun, dust, dew and whirlwinds, and awkward postures while sneezing, eating, sleeping or coughing. Avoid the company of wild, horned and biting animals and of mean, c","chapter":2},{"problem":"Timing: what to avoid at dusk and dawn","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"—","guidance":"The junctions of the day, the meeting of night and sunrise and of day and dusk, are treated as sensitive times. The text advises against taking food, having sex, sleeping, studying or revising at these junctions. These transitional hours are better kept for quiet, settled activity. The broader teaching is that timing matters as much as the action itself: the same act can help or harm depending on when it is done, which is why the daily routine fixes certain things to certain parts of the day.","chapter":2},{"problem":"Rest and sleep without overstraining","category":"Sleep","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Sleep and rest are protected within the routine. The text warns that staying awake late night after night, like over-exercising or over-indulging, wears the body down and leads to breakdown. It teaches stopping the activity of body, speech and mind before exhaustion sets in, so that rest comes before depletion rather than after collapse. Sound sleep is also listed as one of the benefits of daily oil massage. In short, regular adequate sleep and timely rest are treated as active supports for health, especially for keeping Vata steady, not as time taken away from productive life.","chapter":2},{"problem":"The reward of a disciplined daily routine","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"The chapter closes with its promise. A person who keeps reflecting on how each day and night is passing, and who steadily chooses the right path, never becomes a victim of sorrow. Whoever follows this routine of good conduct and daily care gains long life, health, wealth, good reputation and lasting well-being. The point is that the many small habits, waking early, caring for the body, moderate exercise, balanced food, timely rest, kind speech and steady conduct, add up over time into a resilient, contented life. Consistency, not any single practice, is what carries the benefit.","chapter":2},{"problem":"What to eat and do in early winter (Hemanta)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"In early winter the cold traps body heat inside, so your digestive fire runs strong and will start to feed on your own tissues if you underfeed it. Eat heartily and lean on sweet, sour and salty foods: meat soups enriched with fat, dairy and sugarcane preparations, fresh grains, wheat and black-gram dishes. Start the day with warm oil massage (abhyanga) using a Vata-pacifying oil, giving extra attention to the head and forehead, then do gentle exercise to about half your strength, bathe, and warm yourself with sunshine and a fire. Use warm water for washing, dress in warm layers, keep your feet covered, and stay in warm rooms. Intimacy is wel","chapter":3},{"problem":"Late winter care (Shishira)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Late winter follows the same plan as early winter, just applied more firmly, because this is the coldest and driest stretch and it falls inside the sun's drying half of the year. Keep the body warmer, more oiled and better nourished than before. Continue sweet, sour and salty foods and heavier, warming meals, keep up the oil massage, stay in warm surroundings, cover the feet, and avoid cold and dry exposure. Think of it as winter care turned up a notch to match the sharper cold and dryness.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Spring regimen (Vasanta)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"In spring the Kapha that quietly piled up over winter melts in the warming sun, floods the body, dulls digestion and can bring on illness. Your job is to clear the excess. Favour light, dry, Kapha-reducing foods, such as older barley, wheat and honey, and avoid heavy, cold, oily, sour and sweet foods along with daytime sleep, since all of those feed Kapha. Use dry powder massage and regular exercise. Under proper guidance, gentle Kapha-clearing therapies like therapeutic vomiting (Vamana) and nasal cleansing (Nasya) may help. Bathe and apply light cooling pastes like sandalwood and camphor, and spend pleasant midday hours in fragrant, breezy ","chapter":3},{"problem":"Summer regimen (Greeshma)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Summer sun is at its harshest, Kapha keeps dropping and Vata climbs, so energy runs low. Eat sweet, light, cool, slightly oily and liquid foods, and cut back on salty, pungent and sour foods, heavy exercise and sun exposure. Cold grain drinks with a little sugar after a cool bath, cold milk, and water kept cool in earthen pots all suit the season. Keep alcohol to a minimum and well diluted, because too much brings swelling, weakness, burning and confusion. By day stay in cool, shaded, breezy places near water and fragrant cooling mists; at night sleep in the open moonlight in light clothing, with sandalwood paste and gentle fanning. It is bes","chapter":3},{"problem":"Monsoon / rainy-season care (Varsha)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"combination","guidance":"During the rains your digestive fire is at its weakest, already worn down by summer and further upset by damp, cold winds, dirty rainwater and sourness in the air, which stir up all three doshas at once. Protect and rekindle digestion and keep the doshas settled. Eat old grains, well-spiced meat soups and light dishes that are mildly sour, salty and oily with a touch of honey, and drink only rain or deep-well water that has been well boiled. Stay dry and comfortable: use perfumes, keep clothes pleasantly scented, live in the drier upper part of the house, and travel by vehicle rather than walking through the wet. Avoid river water, heavy chil","chapter":3},{"problem":"Autumn regimen (Sharath)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"In autumn the body has grown used to the cool rains and then meets the sun's returning heat, so the Pitta that built up during the monsoon flares. Settle it with bitter, sweet and astringent tastes in light, easy meals, such as rice, green gram, amla and gentle meats. A classic seasonal drink is Hamsodaka, water warmed by the day's sun and cooled by the night's moon over many clear nights, considered pure and balancing and neither clogging nor drying. Spend cool evenings in the moonlight with a light sandalwood paste. Avoid rich curds, oils and fats, strong liquor, overeating, harsh sun, daytime sleep and the eastern breeze. Under guidance, p","chapter":3},{"problem":"Matching tastes and food qualities to the season","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"A quick seasonal taste guide: emphasise sweet, sour and salty tastes in the two winters and the rains; bitter, pungent and astringent in spring; mostly sweet in summer; and sweet, bitter and astringent in autumn. For the quality of food, keep it dry and low in fat during autumn and spring, cool during summer and the tail end of the rains, and warm in the other seasons. The ideal is to include all six tastes every single day for steady health, and simply give extra weight to the tastes the current season calls for rather than dropping any taste entirely.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Why the body weakens in some seasons: Adana, Visarga and digestive fire","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","guidance":"The year has two halves that explain much of how you feel. In the sun's northern, drying course (Adana Kala, covering late winter, spring and summer) the sun and wind steadily pull moisture and strength out of the earth and the body, so energy tends to run lower and the harsher tastes dominate. In the southern, moistening course (Visarga Kala, covering the rains, autumn and early winter) rain, cloud and cool winds restore moisture, so strength tends to be higher and the gentler building tastes dominate. Your digestive fire tracks this too: strongest in the cold seasons, weakest in summer and the rains. Practically, eat heavier and nourish mor","chapter":3},{"problem":"Easing between seasons (Ritu-sandhi transition weeks)","category":"Seasonal","dosha":"combination","guidance":"The final seven days of one season and the opening seven days of the next form a transition seam. During these two weeks, wind down the routine of the season that is ending bit by bit and bring in the new season's routine gradually, rather than switching everything overnight. The body needs time to adapt, and a sudden change, dropping old habits abruptly or jumping straight into new foods and routines, creates a mismatch (asatmya) that can itself make you unwell. Go slow and let your diet, clothing and activity shift step by step as the weather turns.","chapter":3},{"problem":"Natural urges you must never suppress","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"There are thirteen natural urges the body should be allowed to release and never force back: passing gas, stool and urine, sneezing, thirst, hunger, sleep, cough, the heavy breathing after exertion, yawning, tears, vomiting, and ejaculation. Each one is the body clearing or regulating something, and blocking it pushes Vata, the force that governs downward flow, out of its normal path, which is why suppression tends to cause bloating, pain, headaches, constipation, breathing and heart complaints and general discomfort. The practical rule is simple: attend to these needs when they arise rather than holding them in. When problems have already de","chapter":4},{"problem":"Harm from holding in gas, stool and urine (and how it is managed)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"When you hold back gas, Vata that should move downward is forced upward, bringing abdominal bloating and distension, pain, tiredness, constipation, trouble passing urine, dimmed vision and weakened digestion. Holding back stool can add cramping calf pain, runny nose, headache, a tight heavy chest and foul breath, while holding back urine can bring body pain, urinary stones and pain in the bladder and genital area. The guidance is to never postpone these needs. If complaints have set in, helpful measures are oil massage (abhyanga), warm tub or sitz baths, sweating therapy, suppositories, enema (basti), and foods that ease the bowels. For urine","chapter":4},{"problem":"Suppressed sneeze, yawn, belch, cough or heavy breathing","category":"Prevention","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Stifling a sneeze can bring headache, weak senses, neck stiffness and facial palsy; it is relieved by measures that let the sneeze come out and settle Vata, such as inhaling medicated smoke, nasal drops, briefly looking toward bright light, and massage with oleation and sweating. Holding back a yawn causes similar trouble and needs the same Vata-balancing care. Suppressing a belch can cause loss of appetite, tremors, constipation, chest tightness, bloating, cough and hiccups. Holding back a cough only worsens it and can lead to breathing difficulty, poor appetite, heart trouble and wasting, so it should be allowed and treated with normal coug","chapter":4},{"problem":"Suppressing thirst, hunger or sleep","category":"Daily Routine","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Ignoring thirst can lead to dryness and wasting, weakness, deafness, fainting, confusion and heart trouble, so respond with cooling measures like cool drinks and a cool bath. Ignoring hunger can cause body ache, loss of appetite, weakness, weight loss, colicky pain and dizziness; the right response is a light, warm, lightly oiled meal, not a heavy one, because fasting lowers digestive power and raises Vata and a big meal afterward weakens digestion further, while warm oily food soothes the stomach and keeps the digestive fire steady. Ignoring sleep brings confusion, a heavy head and eyes, laziness, yawning and body ache, and is set right by r","chapter":4},{"problem":"Suppressing tears, vomiting or ejaculation","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Holding back tears can cause a blocked or running nose, eye disorders, painful heart conditions, neck stiffness, poor appetite, dizziness and abdominal masses; gentle comfort such as rest, sleep and warm friendly conversation helps. Holding back vomiting mainly disturbs Pitta and can bring spreading skin diseases, rashes and other skin disorders, eye problems, itching, anaemia, fever, cough, breathing difficulty, nausea and facial pigmentation; care focuses on clearing Pitta through gargling, medicated smoke, eating dry food and then inducing vomiting, exercise, bloodletting for skin issues, and purgation. Holding back ejaculation can cause d","chapter":4},{"problem":"Urges you should hold back (greed, envy, anger)","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"While the body's physical urges should be allowed to flow, the mental and moral urges are the ones to deliberately rein in. Hold back greed, envy, hatred, jealousy, and unlawful or excessive craving and attraction. Mastering the senses and restraining these impulses is treated as a genuine part of staying well, not just good manners, because unchecked they drive wrong actions that lead to suffering and illness. In everyday terms, let the body do what it naturally needs, but govern the mind with self-control, patience and an even temper.","chapter":4},{"problem":"Clearing doshas at the right time (Shodhana / Panchakarma) and recovering after","category":"Detox","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Waste and doshas should be cleared at their proper seasons before they accumulate, because stored toxins can aggravate and, at worst, become dangerous. There is a key difference between approaches: doshas only pacified by fasting and digestive therapies can flare up again later, while doshas truly expelled by purification therapy (Shodhana, the Panchakarma procedures) tend not to return. A good seasonal rhythm is to expel what builds in winter during spring, what builds in summer during the rains, and what builds in the rains during autumn. After proper cleansing, rebuild gradually with nourishing foods like fast-maturing rice, wheat, green g","chapter":4},{"problem":"Everyday conduct that keeps you free of disease","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"The secret of lasting health is a steady, sensible daily life. Eat wholesome food and keep wholesome routines every day, weigh the good and bad of things before acting, and do not become overly attached to sensory pleasures. Control the senses, learn from past experience, and understand your environment, the season and your own nature so you can adapt. Follow good conduct and healthy habits, keep a calm and peaceful mind, practise charity, honesty, patience and fairness, and keep good company. Clear the doshas at their proper seasons so seasonal illness never takes hold. Remember too that some illnesses come from outside, from microbes and un","chapter":4},{"problem":"Rainwater — the best water and how to judge it","category":"Drinks","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Fresh rainwater caught in a clean vessel is treated as the finest of all waters. Having met sunlight, moonlight and wind, it is light to digest, thin, cooling, almost tasteless and soothing to the heart and mind. A traditional quality test is to pour it over boiled rice on a clean silver plate; good water neither makes the rice soggy nor changes its colour. Drink it fresh, as it loses its virtues on standing, and remember its goodness still depends on the season and place where it fell. In the rains it is the best drink, better than river water. In modern conditions, pollution has taken away much of rainwater's natural purity, so judgement is","chapter":5},{"problem":"Water to avoid — contaminated and unsafe water","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Refuse water that is muddy or full of algae, weeds and fallen leaves, water shut away from sun and wind, a mixture of stale and fresh, water that is thick and hard to digest, frothy, wormy, naturally warm, or so icy it sets the teeth on edge. Out-of-season rain, the very first rains, and any water fouled by spiders, saliva, urine or droppings are all unsafe. Stagnant river or pond water that has lost its movement is said to breed intestinal worms and to foster diseases of the heart, throat and head, so moving, clean, sun-and-wind-exposed water is always preferred.","chapter":5},{"problem":"River and well water — how the source changes the water","category":"Drinks","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Water takes on the character of the land it flows through. Sweet, clean rivers running west into the Arabian Sea are considered wholesome, while east-flowing rivers are less suitable. Mountain streams that churn and tumble over rocks stay healthy, but once they stagnate they can cause worms and swellings. Water from different hill ranges carries different faults, ranging from piles and belly swelling to skin disease and anaemia, and sea water disturbs all three doshas except in the autumn month when it is briefly considered safe. Well and pond water simply mirrors the surrounding terrain, so the setting matters as much as the source.","chapter":5},{"problem":"How much water to drink and when","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"People with weak digestion, abdominal tumours, anaemia, a swollen belly, diarrhoea, piles, duodenal disease or dropsy should drink very little plain water or none. Even healthy people are advised to keep water modest in every season except summer and autumn. Around meals the timing matters: sipping a little during the meal is the balanced habit, drinking heavily after a meal tends to add weight, and drinking a lot before a meal tends to thin the body and weaken it. The practical message is to drink with the meal in small amounts rather than loading up before or after.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Cold water — when it helps","category":"Drinks","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Cold water is soothing in heat-driven and Pitta states. It settles the morning-after feeling of alcohol, relieves exhaustion, fainting, vomiting, fatigue, giddiness and strong thirst, calms burning sensations and sun heat, helps bleeding tendencies and Pitta-dominant conditions, and is used to counter the effects of poison. It is the natural choice when the body feels overheated or overwrought.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Warm and boiled water — the digestive aid","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Hot water kindles appetite, helps digestion, is kind to the throat, stays light, and cleanses the bladder. It relieves hiccup, bloating, aggravated Vata and Kapha, recent fever, cough, a build-up of undigested residue, running nose, breathlessness and pain in the flanks, and it suits the days of cleansing therapy. Water that is boiled and then cooled does not clog the tissue channels and remains light, which makes it a good choice in Pitta conditions. Water left standing overnight is best avoided, as it is said to disturb all three doshas.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Tender coconut water","category":"Drinks","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Tender coconut water is sweet, lightly oily, cooling, light to digest and mildly strengthening. It quenches thirst, calms both Pitta and Vata, gently kindles appetite and helps clear the urinary bladder. It is a gentle, refreshing drink, well suited to hot weather and to conditions where the body runs warm.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Milk — general qualities and who it suits","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Milk as a class is sweet both on the tongue and after digestion, oily, cooling and heavy. It nourishes all the body tissues, builds vital essence (ojas), is mildly aphrodisiac, and calms Vata and Pitta while increasing Kapha. This makes it restorative for the weak, the depleted and those with dryness or heat, but less suited to people who are already heavy, congested or slow in digestion. Milk is always better boiled, since raw milk tends to clog the channels and cause undigested residue.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Cow's milk","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Cow's milk is the most prized milk. It promotes long life, rejuvenates and slows ageing, sharpens the intellect, strengthens the body, helps breast-milk flow and eases the bowels. It is used for exhaustion, giddiness, intoxication, breathlessness and cough, severe hunger and thirst, long-standing fever and painful urination, is good for someone emaciated after a chest injury, and helps calm bleeding disorders. Boiled and taken warm, it is a broadly restorative daily nourishment for depleted people.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Buffalo, goat and camel milk — which milk for whom","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Different milks suit different people. Buffalo milk is heavier and cooling and suits a person with very strong digestion who sleeps poorly. Goat milk is light to digest, because the goat roams and eats sharp, light fodder, and it helps wasting, fever, breathlessness, Pitta-type bleeding and diarrhoea. Camel milk is slightly dry, warm, salty and light, kindles digestion and is useful in Vata and Kapha states, worms, swelling, belly disease and piles. Ewe milk is warm, hard on the heart and raises Pitta and Kapha, and milk of single-hoofed animals like the horse is very hot and dulling, so both are used sparingly.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Curd (Dadhi) — benefits and strict rules","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Curd is sour in taste and after digestion, absorbent, heavy and warming, and it calms Vata. It increases fat, semen, strength, Kapha, bleeding tendency, digestive fire and swelling, and whets the appetite, so it helps loss of appetite, recurrent fever, running nose, painful urination and malabsorption. But the rules around it are firm: do not eat curd at night, do not heat it, do not take it with green-gram soup, and never combine it with honey, ghee, sugar or sour fruit. It should also not be eaten every single day, because daily use can bring on or worsen fever, bleeding disorders, skin disease, anaemia and dizziness.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Buttermilk (Takra) for digestion","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Buttermilk is curd that has been churned with the fat removed. It is light, sour and astringent, kindles digestion and calms both Kapha and Vata. It is a go-to for swelling, belly disease, piles, malabsorption and loose motions, urinary trouble, poor appetite, enlarged spleen, abdominal lumps, the heaviness that follows eating too much ghee, chronic low-grade poisoning and anaemia. Being light and digestive, it is one of the most useful everyday drinks for a sluggish gut. The watery whey (mastu) left over is similar: it keeps fluids moving through the channels, cleanses them, relieves constipation and is light.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Fresh butter (Navanita)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Fresh butter is aphrodisiac and cooling, improves complexion, strength and digestive power, is absorbent so it helps diarrhoea, and balances Vata, Pitta and the blood. It is used for wasting conditions, piles, facial palsy and cough. Butter churned directly from milk, rather than from curd, is particularly absorbent and useful in bleeding disorders and eye diseases. It is a gentler, cooling fat compared with the deeper action of ghee.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Ghee (clarified butter) — the best of fats","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Ghee is praised as the finest of all fatty substances. It improves intellect, memory, digestive power, longevity, vigour and eyesight, and suits children, the elderly, the delicate and those who wish for children. It helps disorders of Vata and Pitta origin, poison, insanity, wasting and fever, and is cooling and youth-preserving. A key idea is that ghee takes on and carries the power of whatever herbs it is cooked with, so it becomes the base for countless medicinal preparations. Old ghee is reserved for intoxication, epilepsy, fainting and diseases of the head, ear, eye and genitals, and it both cleanses and heals wounds.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Thick milk products — kilata, piyusha, kurchika","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Concentrated dairy such as kilata (the solid from heated curd or buttermilk), piyusha (the first milk for about three days after calving), kurchika and morana are strengthening and increase semen, sleep and Kapha. The trade-off is that they cause constipation, are heavy to digest and tend to disturb the doshas, so they are rich building foods rather than everyday ones. Overall, cow's milk and cow's ghee are rated the best dairy, while ewe's milk and ghee are rated the least.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Sugarcane juice and its products","category":"Energy","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Fresh sugarcane juice is laxative, heavy, oily, nourishing and weight-building, cooling, mildly aphrodisiac, increases urine and Kapha, and helps bleeding disorders. For best quality it should be chewed or pressed cleanly, because juice crushed together with the root, shoot tip and wormy parts becomes impure and then causes burning, indigestion and constipation. Among its solid products, well-purified jaggery does not raise Kapha much and increases stool and urine, old jaggery is good for the heart, and fresh jaggery raises Kapha and causes indigestion. Refined sugars improve in quality from brown sugar to sugar candy to white crystal sugar, ","chapter":5},{"problem":"Honey (Madhu) — benefits and the rule never to heat it","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Honey is good for the eyes, scrapes and breaks up hardened masses and excess Kapha, relieves thirst and calms Kapha. It helps poison, hiccup, bleeding, diabetes and urinary disease, skin disease, worms, vomiting, breathlessness, cough and diarrhoea, and it cleanses wounds and helps them close and heal quickly. It is drying and astringent-sweet and slightly raises Vata. The famous and strict rule is never to heat honey: it is said to turn harmful when heated, when taken by someone who is very overheated, in hot weather or a hot climate, or mixed into hot food. The one exception is using it warm to induce vomiting or in a decoction enema, becau","chapter":5},{"problem":"Sesame oil and other oils","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"An oil generally carries the nature of the seed it comes from, and sesame oil is considered the chief of all oils. It cleanses and detoxifies the skin, is good for the eyes, penetrates deep into the tissues, is warm and reduces Kapha, and uniquely suits both the obese and the thin. It relieves constipation and worms, and when processed with herbs it becomes a base for treating many diseases. Other oils have their own uses: mustard oil is pungent, hot and penetrating and reduces Kapha but can irritate; neem oil is bitter, mildly hot and antimicrobial for skin disease; vibhitaka oil is sweet, cooling and good for hair; and linseed and safflower","chapter":5},{"problem":"Castor oil (Eranda taila)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Castor oil is bitter, pungent and sweet in taste, laxative, heavy, deeply penetrating, hot and strong-smelling. It is a classic Vata remedy, used to produce purgation, ease pain and reduce swelling. It helps hernia and scrotal enlargement, abdominal lumps, Vata-Kapha diseases, ascites and intermittent fever, and relieves pain and swelling of the waist, genitals, abdomen and back. The red-seed variety of castor oil is even more penetrating and heating. Because it works deeply on Vata, it is valued across many conditions but used with care given its strength.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Alcoholic drinks (Madya) — general qualities and cautions","category":"General","dosha":"combination","guidance":"As a group, wines kindle digestion, improve taste, penetrate deeply and are hot, give satisfaction and nourishment, and taste slightly sweet, bitter and pungent, turning sour after digestion. They are laxative and slightly astringent, said to improve voice, health, talent and complexion, are light, and suit both the over-sleeping and the sleepless and both lean and stout people, clearing the fine channels and calming Vata and Kapha. All of this holds only when they are used wisely; used wrongly they act like poison. Fresh wine is heavy and raises all the doshas, while aged wine reduces them. Wine should not be taken hot, nor during purgation ","chapter":5},{"problem":"Types of wine — sura, arishta, grape and sugarcane wines","category":"General","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Each fermented drink has its own leaning. Sura (a rice beer) helps abdominal lumps, swollen belly, piles and duodenal disease, is lubricating and heavy and increases fat, blood, milk, urine and Kapha. Arishta, a fermented herbal decoction, is the strongest and carries the power of its herbs, treating duodenal disease, anaemia, skin disease, piles, dropsy, fever, lumps, worms and spleen disorder. Grape wine (mardvika) is heart-friendly, sweet, laxative and helps anaemia, diabetes, piles and worms. Sidhu from sugarcane raises Vata and Pitta but reduces Kapha disease, fat and dropsy. Honey wine (madhvasava) breaks up masses and helps diabetes, n","chapter":5},{"problem":"Sour fermented grain liquids (Dhanyamla, Sauviraka)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Dhanyamla is made by fermenting the water in which grains and pulses were washed or lightly cooked. It is purgative, penetrating and hot, raises Pitta, feels cool to the touch, relieves fatigue and exhaustion, sharpens appetite, eases bladder pain, is heart-friendly and light, and balances Vata and Kapha. It is also used as a medicated enema fluid. Sauviraka and tushodaka, prepared from barley with and without husk, are similar and help worms, heart disease, abdominal lumps, piles and anaemia. These sour liquids were common digestive tonics in older times.","chapter":5},{"problem":"Rice — qualities and which variety is best","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Rice as a food is sweet in taste and after digestion, oily, mildly aphrodisiac, light to digest, cooling, slightly astringent, increases urine and causes only a little stool, which makes it suitable for daily eating. Among the many kinds, red rice (Raktashali) is considered the best because it quenches thirst and balances all three doshas; big-grained rice comes next, then kalama, and so on down the traditional list. Rice that ripens in sixty days (Shashtika) is especially valued as oily, absorbent, light, steadying, cooling and tridosha-balancing. Late or inferior varieties tend to be hot, heavy and sour after digestion and raise Kapha and P","chapter":6},{"problem":"Millets and grass grains (kangu, kodrava, shyamaka)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Grains from grass-like plants such as kangu, kodrava, neevara and shyamaka are cooling, light to digest and scraping in action; they raise Vata somewhat but settle Kapha and Pitta, which makes them useful light staples for heavy or congested conditions. Among them priyangu helps knit fractures and is nourishing though heavy, while koradusha is strongly absorbent, cooling and regarded as anti-poisonous. Because they are drying and Vata-raising, they suit Kapha types better than those who are already dry or windy.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Barley (Yava)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Barley is dry, cooling and heavy, sweet in taste, laxative, and it increases the bulk of stool and flatus while being mildly aphrodisiac and steadying for the body. It reduces fat and helps urinary disorders, Pitta-Kapha imbalances, running nose, breathlessness and asthma, cough, stiffness of the thighs, throat disease and skin disease. Its drying, reducing nature makes it a classic grain for Kapha-heavy and overweight conditions, though its heaviness and dryness mean Vata-prone people should take it prepared with care.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Wheat (Godhuma)","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Wheat is mildly aphrodisiac, cooling, heavy, oily and enlivening. It calms both Vata and Pitta, helps knit fractures and heal wounds, is sweet, steadies the body and gently moves the bowels, and is considered fit for daily eating. Being nourishing and grounding, it suits dry, depleted and Vata-type constitutions, while its heaviness means it is less ideal for those with sluggish digestion or heavy Kapha.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Pulses and legumes — general qualities","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Green gram, toor dal, lentils and their relatives share a common nature: they tend to constipate and produce gas, are astringent-sweet in taste, absorbent, pungent after digestion, cooling and light to digest. As a group they reduce fat, Kapha, blood and Pitta, and are even used as external pastes and in bathing. This makes them good for Kapha and Pitta reduction, but their drying, gas-forming tendency means Vata-prone people should cook them well with spices and fats. Fresh-harvested grain clogs the channels; grain a year old, dehusked or properly fried is lighter and easier to digest.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Green gram, black gram and other specific pulses","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Among the pulses, green gram (mudga) is the best and raises Vata only slightly, which is why it is the standard dal for the sick and the recovering. Round pea (kalaya) raises Vata and dryness and makes more stool, and rajamasha is dry, heavy and Vata-raising. Black gram (masha) is the opposite extreme: oily, strengthening, heavy, hot, sweet, laxative, it calms Vata and strongly builds semen and sexual strength, but it raises Kapha and Pitta and increases stool, so it is a building food for the depleted rather than an everyday one. Horse gram is hot and reducing. The fruits of kakandola and atmagupta (kapikacchu) resemble black gram in action.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Horse gram (Kulattha)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Horse gram is hot in potency and sour after digestion. It cleanses semen and is used for urinary stones, breathlessness and asthma, running nose, cough and Kapha-Vata diseases, and its soup specifically normalises the downward movement of Vata and helps abdominal lumps and pains of the groin region. The important caution is that it increases bleeding disorders, so it should be avoided in conditions like heavy menstrual bleeding or any Pitta-type haemorrhage.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Sesame seed (Tila)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Sesame is hot in potency yet feels cool to the touch, is good for the skin and hair, strengthening and heavy, gives little urine, turns pungent after digestion, sharpens intellect and digestive fire, and raises Kapha and Pitta. It is a building, grounding seed well suited to Vata depletion and to nourishing the hair and skin, but its heaviness and heating action mean it should be used in moderation by Pitta and Kapha types. Linseed (uma) and safflower seeds are similar, oily and hot, but are noted as not good for vision or semen.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Rice preparations — manda, peya, vilepi, odana","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Rice cooked with water passes through four stages of increasing thickness and heaviness: manda is the thin water-like liquid drained off first, peya is a little thicker but still liquid, vilepi has more grain and less liquid, and odana is solid cooked rice. They get heavier in that order, so manda is lightest and best for the most fragile digestion. Manda moves stool and flatus, relieves thirst and exhaustion, clears leftover doshas and is the ideal food right after cleansing therapy. Peya relieves hunger, thirst, weakness, belly disease and fever and rekindles appetite. Vilepi holds in fluids, is heart-friendly and suits ulcers, eye disease,","chapter":6},{"problem":"Soups — meat soup, green-gram soup, horse-gram soup","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Meat soup (mamsa rasa) is nourishing, satisfying, aphrodisiac, good for the eyes and helps heal ulcers and wounds, making it a strong restorative broth for the depleted. Green-gram soup is light and wholesome, especially good for those recovering from cleansing therapy and for ulcers and diseases of the throat and eyes. Horse-gram soup normalises the downward movement of Vata and is used for abdominal lumps and pains of the groin. Light soups like these are a gentle way to nourish and medicate at the same time.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Processed and fried foods, and sprouted grains — what to limit","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Several processed items are flagged as hard to digest and dosha-aggravating, including sesame cake, dried leafy vegetables, germinated or sprouted grains and certain fried dough balls; sprouted grains in particular are not recommended because they are heavy, harm the eyes and disturb the doshas. Oil-cake residue (pinyaka) causes giddiness, dryness and indigestion and dulls vision. Roasted corn flour (saktu) is nourishing and strengthening but has strict rules: take it with water, not at night, not twice a day, not on its own without other food, not after a meal, and never in a hard-to-chew or excessive amount. As a general cooking note, food ","chapter":6},{"problem":"Flaked and puffed rice (laja, prithuka) and roasted flour","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Puffed or parched paddy (laja) relieves thirst, vomiting and diarrhoea, helps diabetes and obesity, reduces Kapha, cough and Pitta, kindles appetite, is light to digest and cooling, which makes it a useful light food in heat and congestion. Flaked paddy (prithuka), made by boiling and pounding paddy, is the opposite: heavy to digest, strengthening, Kapha-increasing and constipating. Roasted barley flour (dhana) stays long in the stomach and can cause indigestion, is dry, satisfying and scraping. These forms differ sharply, so the preparation matters as much as the grain.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Jangala (dry-land) meats — the lightest, healthiest meats","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Meats are grouped by habitat, and the dry-land (jangala) group, which includes deer, rabbit and the ground-scratching and pecking birds, is considered the best. These meats are cooling in potency, light to digest and mildly constipating, and they are especially good in mixed-dosha fevers where Pitta is high. Rabbit (shasha) in particular enhances appetite and is cooling. Because they are the lightest meats, they are the preferred animal foods for the sick, the recovering and anyone needing easily digested protein.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Poultry and game birds — partridge, peacock, fowl","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"The quail and partridge group are building meats: slightly hot, heavy, oily and body-strengthening. Partridge (tittiri) is rated especially good for increasing intelligence, digestive power, strength and semen, improving complexion and calming Vata-dominant mixed-dosha states. Peacock, though generally not rated highly, is said to benefit the ears, voice, eyes and healthy ageing. Wild fowl is aphrodisiac, whereas domesticated fowl is heavier and raises Kapha. Black partridge (krakara) sharpens intellect and digestion and is good for the heart. These are nourishing meats for depletion rather than light everyday ones.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Goat, sheep, cow, buffalo and pig meat compared","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Goat meat is the balanced choice: not too cooling, matching the make-up of the human body, so it does not clog the channels or aggravate the doshas, and it nourishes and adds healthy weight. Sheep meat is the opposite in nature and simply fattening. Cow meat is described as treating dry cough, exhaustion, excess hunger, intermittent fever, chronic nasal catarrh and emaciation from independent Vata increase. Buffalo meat is hot, heavy, and induces sleep, strength and bulk. Pig relieves fatigue and builds taste, semen and strength. Fish generally increase Kapha, and some kinds disturb all three doshas. The overall rule is to use freshly killed,","chapter":6},{"problem":"Leafy greens — general rule and the best and worst","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Among plant parts, leaves are lightest, then flowers, then unripe fruit, then stalks, then tubers, which are heaviest. Most leafy greens, especially the bitter ones, are cooling, pungent after digestion, hold in body fluids, raise Vata and reduce Kapha and Pitta. Jivanti is rated the best of the leafy vegetables, being good for the eyes, balancing to all doshas, sweet and cooling, while mustard greens (sarshapa) are rated the worst because they bind stool and urine and raise all three doshas. Cooking greens with oil or ghee and taking out their juice reduces their tendency to aggravate the doshas.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Bitter and medicinal greens (patola, vasa, bitter gourd, kakamachi)","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"The bitter greens are cooling and reducing. Patola (pointed gourd) is good for the heart, anti-worm, sweet after digestion and improves taste. Vasa cures vomiting, cough and especially bleeding disorders. Bitter gourd (karavella) is bitter, kindles digestion and strongly reduces Kapha and Pitta. Black nightshade (kakamachi) balances the three doshas, helps skin disease, is mildly aphrodisiac and rejuvenating and moves the bowels. Wood sorrel (changeri) is sour, kindles digestion and helps duodenal disease and piles. These greens are chosen for Kapha and Pitta reduction, digestion and worm and skin problems.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Brinjal (eggplant)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Brinjal is pungent, bitter and slightly sweet, hot in potency and mildly alkaline. It reduces both Kapha and Vata, kindles the digestive fire and improves taste and appetite, and notably it does not aggravate Pitta much. This makes it a warming, appetising vegetable well suited to weak digestion and Kapha-Vata sluggishness.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Ash gourd and the gourd family","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Ash gourd (kushmanda) is rated the best of the creeper vegetables. It calms both Vata and Pitta, cleanses the urinary bladder and is aphrodisiac, making it a prized cooling and strengthening food. Tender gourds of the cucumber-melon family soothe Pitta and are cooling when young but turn opposite when ripe. Cucumber (trapusa) is diuretic. Many gourds raise Kapha and Vata and sit heavy, so they are best eaten young and well cooked.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Radish (Mulaka)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Radish is a vegetable whose value depends entirely on its age. Tender radish, before it develops a strong taste, is slightly alkaline, balances all the doshas, is light, hot in potency, and cures abdominal lumps, cough, respiratory conditions, ulcers, diseases of the eye and throat, hoarseness, bloating and nasal catarrh. Large, mature radish is the opposite: pungent, hard to digest, aggravating to all three doshas and clogging. When cooked with fats it calms Vata, while dried radish increases the doshas. So choose it young and cook it with oil or ghee.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Pungent kitchen greens — holy basil, drumstick, mint family","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Green pungent herbs such as holy basil (tulasi), drumstick (sigru), surasa and their relatives are absorbent when fresh, cause some burning during digestion, are pungent and drying, hot in potency, good for the heart and mind, and they kindle hunger and taste and destroy worms. The caution is that they can harm vision and semen and cause a slight rise in the doshas. Holy basil (tulasi) specifically relieves hiccup, cough, poison, asthma, flank pain and bad breath. These are warming, appetising seasonings rather than bulk foods.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Garlic (Lashuna), onion and yam for piles","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Garlic is strongly penetrating, hot, pungent in taste and after digestion, laxative, good for the heart and hair, heavy, aphrodisiac, oily, appetising, bone-knitting, strengthening and a recognised rejuvenator; it treats leucoderma, skin disease, abdominal lumps, piles, diabetes, worms, Kapha-Vata disease, hiccup, nasal catarrh, asthma and cough, but it strongly vitiates blood and Pitta, so Pitta types should be careful. Onion (palandu) is a milder, weaker version that raises Pitta less. Carrot (grinjanaka) suits piles of Kapha-Vata origin but not Pitta-dominant people, and yam (surana) kindles digestion, reduces Kapha and is especially good ","chapter":6},{"problem":"Grapes (Draksha) — the best fruit","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Grapes are considered the finest of all fruits. They are aphrodisiac, good for the eyes, gently move urine and stool, sweet in taste and after digestion, oily, slightly astringent, cooling and heavy. They soothe disorders of Vata, Pitta and blood, improve the taste in the mouth, and relieve intoxication, thirst, cough, fever, breathlessness, hoarseness, injury to the lungs and chronic respiratory disease. This broad, gentle, restorative action is why grapes top the fruit list.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Pomegranate (Dadima)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Pomegranate subdues greatly increased Pitta in particular, and also soothes the other doshas, while being sweet. The sour variety still does not raise Pitta much, is only mildly hot and calms Vata and Kapha. All pomegranate is good for the heart, light to digest, oily, absorbent (so it checks loose motions) and stimulating to appetite and digestion. It is an unusually balancing fruit, suiting Pitta conditions yet gentle on the other doshas.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Sweet building fruits and fruits by ripeness (banana, mango, bael, jamun)","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"A large group of sweet, cooling, heavy, oily fruits such as banana, dates, jackfruit and coconut build the body, relieve burning, chest injury and bleeding, and are sweet after digestion, but they sit long in the stomach and increase Kapha and semen. Ripeness changes a fruit's action sharply: bael (bilva) is good unripe (kindles digestion, calms Vata and Kapha) but troublesome when ripe; mango is Vata-, blood- and Pitta-raising when unripe and Vata-calming but Kapha- and semen-increasing when ripe. Jamun is cooling, absorbent, drying and strongly Vata-aggravating. Sour fruits like amla and kola mostly aid digestion and move the bowels. Lakuch","chapter":6},{"problem":"Salts — rock salt and the others","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"All salts are penetrating, soften and ease the movement of stool, calm Vata, aid digestion, and tend to raise Kapha and Pitta. Rock salt (saindhava) is the best of them: mildly sweet, aphrodisiac, good for the heart and mind, balancing to all three doshas, light, not overly hot and non-burning, which is why it is the preferred salt for daily and medicinal use. Sochal salt (sauvarchala) is light, digestive, fragrant and clears belching and constipation; bida salt moves Kapha and Vata both up and down and relieves bloating, colic and heaviness; while common sea salt is heavier and raises Kapha more.","chapter":6},{"problem":"Triphala and the myrobalans (haritaki, amalaki, vibhitaki)","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","guidance":"The three myrobalans together make Triphala, regarded as a leading rejuvenator that helps eye disease, heals wounds, clears skin disease, excess tissue moisture, obesity, diabetes and aggravated Kapha and blood. Haritaki on its own is astringent, sweet after digestion, drying, light, appetising and digestive, sharpens the mind, best preserves youth, is hot in potency and laxative, lengthens life and treats a very wide range of Kapha-Vata disorders. Amalaki is similar but cooling and calms Pitta and Kapha, and vibhitaki (aksha) is cooling, good for hair and pungent after digestion. Together they are a cornerstone of preventive and rejuvenating","chapter":6},{"problem":"Warming spices — pepper, long pepper, ginger, trikatu","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"The warming spices are the main digestive and Kapha-reducing condiments. Black pepper (maricha) is pungent in taste and after digestion, reduces Kapha and is light. Long pepper (pippali) when dry is aphrodisiac, pungent, sweet after digestion, reduces Vata and Kapha and helps asthma and cough, and is laxative, but it should not be used in excess or for long periods without a proper rejuvenation regimen. Ginger (nagara, and fresh ardraka) kindles appetite, is aphrodisiac, absorbent, heart-friendly, relieves constipation, improves taste, is light, sweet after digestion, oily, hot and reduces Kapha and Vata. Pepper, long pepper and ginger togeth","chapter":6},{"problem":"Asafoetida (Hingu) and the panchamula root groups","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Asafoetida (hingu) calms Vata and Kapha, relieves distension of the abdomen and colic, is pungent in taste and after digestion, enhances taste, hunger and digestion and is light, though it slightly raises Pitta. For deeper treatment, Ayurveda groups several roots into panchamula sets: the greater and lesser panchamula (which together form the dashamula) and the middle, life-giving (jivana) and grass (trina) panchamulas. Broadly, the greater and lesser root groups reduce Kapha and Vata, the jivana group is good for the eyes, aphrodisiac and calms Pitta and Vata, and the trina group reduces Pitta. These are medicinal condiments and decoction ba","chapter":6},{"problem":"Incompatible food combinations (Viruddha ahara)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Some foods are safe on their own but harmful when combined, because together they stir up the Doshas without letting the body clear them. Treat such combinations like a mild poison. Avoid pairing milk with anything sour (sour fruit, curd, buttermilk) and with fish; avoid fish with jaggery or radish; do not mix honey and ghee in equal amounts, do not take honey with hot water, and never heat honey. Skip milk right after eating radish or leafy greens, and do not store ghee in a bronze vessel for more than about ten days. The practical signal is this: if a mixture or a method of cooking makes you bloated, acidic or unwell even though each item a","chapter":7},{"problem":"Safe food, and spotting food that has gone bad or been tampered with","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"Guarding food is the heart of this chapter. Watch for unnatural changes: food that loses its normal colour, smell, taste and texture too fast, turns oddly thick or watery with shiny specks, grows froth, threads or bubbles, or shows strange coloured streaks. Fruit that ripens or rots too quickly, soft foods that turn hard, and foul or sharp smells are all warnings. If in doubt, do not eat it. The ancient fire and animal tests were simply ways to confirm suspicion before eating. The modern takeaway is to keep food clean and covered, eat it fresh, trust the people who prepare it, and throw away anything that looks, smells or tastes off rather th","chapter":7},{"problem":"Changing a diet or habit safely (gradual accustomization)","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"Do not swing suddenly from one extreme to another, because an abrupt change in either direction can itself trigger illness. Drop an unhealthy food or habit a little at a time, roughly a quarter of the amount in each step, and bring in a new healthy one slowly too, adding it over spaced days. This gentle tapering lets the body adjust. Over time the bad influence fades and the good habit takes hold, and the result is steady, robust health rather than the shock and relapse that come from going cold turkey.","chapter":7},{"problem":"Healthy sleep (Nidra) — timing and amount","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Sleep is one of the three pillars of health, and happiness, nourishment, strength and clarity of mind all depend on it. Three faults harm you: sleeping at the wrong time, sleeping too much, and not sleeping enough. Sleep at night, at a regular hour, as much as you genuinely need. Night waking dries the body, while daytime sleep tends to moisten it and raise Kapha and Pitta, so avoid day naps except in summer or if you are genuinely exhausted, ill, very old or very young. If you lost a night's sleep, you may rest the next morning for about half that time, without first eating. For too little sleep, use milk, nourishing meals, oil massage, a wa","chapter":7},{"problem":"The three pillars of life (food, sleep, balanced sex life)","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"The body is held up by three supports, like a house on pillars: Ahara (food), Nidra (sleep) and a balanced, disciplined sex life. When all three are used wisely they sustain health; when any is neglected or overused, health falls. Eat well and at the right time, sleep properly at night, and keep intimacy in moderation, which the text says should be freer in the cold seasons and much more restrained in the rains and heat. Overindulgence drains strength and ages the body early, while balance preserves memory, energy and long life. After intimacy, rest, bathing and cooling drinks help restore vigour.","chapter":7},{"problem":"Suspected food poisoning — first care","category":"General","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Classical Ayurveda treated poisoning in stages by where it acts. Contact with the skin causes itching, burning and eruptions, eased by washing and cooling herbal pastes such as sandalwood and khus. In the mouth it numbs the tongue and burns, eased by medicated gargles. In the stomach and intestines it causes vomiting, bloating, giddiness, loose motions and weakness, for which the texts used controlled emesis and purgation to clear the toxin, followed by soothing, strengthening measures. This is educational background only. Real suspected poisoning is an emergency: stop eating the food, keep it for identification, and get prompt medical help r","chapter":7},{"problem":"How much to eat (the right quantity of food)","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Eat the measured amount that keeps your digestive fire (Agni) working well, because the right quantity is what lets food digest cleanly. As a guide, heavy foods like oily dishes, meat and sweets should fill only about half of your comfortable capacity, while light foods can be a bit more but still stop short of feeling full. The surest test is the result: you ate the right amount if the meal digests without discomfort, heaviness or sleepiness. The best practical rule is to divide the stomach into three parts, fill two with solid food and one with liquid, and leave the last part empty so there is room for movement and digestion. In short, stop","chapter":8},{"problem":"Eating too little or too much","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Both extremes harm you. Eating too little fails to build strength and immunity, leaves the body under-nourished, and over time triggers Vata problems such as weakness and aches. Eating too much suddenly disturbs all three Doshas at once; mixed with undigested food it creates Ama (toxic residue) and can block the gut (causing a stuck, lazy digestion) or force food out violently as vomiting and diarrhoea. The fix is self-control at the table: eat a moderate, measured amount, do not push past comfortable fullness, and if you have eaten too much, lighten the next meal or fast briefly rather than piling more food on top.","chapter":8},{"problem":"Indigestion (Ajirna) — types and what to do","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Indigestion comes in a few forms. Kapha-type (Ama ajirna) brings puffy eyes and cheeks, drooling, nausea, heaviness and belching that tastes of the meal; its remedy is fasting until the gut clears. Vata-type (Vishtabdha) brings belly pain, gas, constipation and weakness; its remedy is warmth and sweating therapy (a warm bath, hot water bottle, light fomentation). Pitta-type (Vidagdha) brings thirst, giddiness, sour burning belches; here lightening and cooling help, and the classics used controlled emesis. For any type, the general advice is gentle: rest quietly, do not eat while still full, drink warm water, and only eat again, in a small, li","chapter":8},{"problem":"Undigested food toxins (Ama) and how to clear them","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Ama is food left half-digested that turns toxic inside you. It builds up from eating incompatible foods, overeating, and eating again before the last meal is digested. The remedy is matched to how much Ama there is. If it is little, simple fasting (Langhana) is enough, just skip a meal and let the gut catch up. If it is moderate, fast and add digestive support such as warm water, ginger and light spiced food (Pachana). If it is heavy and entrenched, a supervised cleansing (Shodhana) is needed to remove it at the root. The guiding principle is lightening, not forcing food down. Once appetite and lightness return, ease back gently with small, w","chapter":8},{"problem":"Bad eating habits (Dushta Ashana)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Three habits are singled out as harmful enough to cause serious disease. Samashana is mixing suitable and unsuitable foods in one meal. Adhyashana is eating a fresh meal before the previous one has digested, the classic cause of Ama. Vishamashana is eating too much or too little, or at the wrong, irregular times. The cure is rhythm and simplicity: eat at regular times, let one meal fully digest (real hunger is the signal) before the next, and keep meals coherent rather than a jumble of clashing items. Also avoid eating when grief-stricken, angry or over-hungry, since a disturbed mind and extreme hunger both spoil digestion.","chapter":8},{"problem":"Rules for a healthy meal (Ahara vidhi)","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"—","guidance":"Eat clean, familiar, wholesome food that is warm, lightly unctuous and easy to digest. Include all six tastes with sweet predominating, and eat neither too fast nor too slowly. Eat only when genuinely hungry, after washing hands, feet and face, in a calm quiet place and in good company, with full attention on the meal rather than screens or arguments. Do not criticise the food and do not talk too much while eating. Favour some liquid or moist food. Avoid reheated food, food fouled with grass or hair, food that is mostly poor vegetables or inferior grains, and anything too hot or too salty. Within the meal, take heavy sweet oily foods first, s","chapter":8},{"problem":"The right time to eat","category":"Digestion","dosha":"—","guidance":"The ideal moment to eat is when the body shows it is ready: after you have passed stool and urine, when the mind is calm, belching is clean without sour taste or smell, hunger is clearly felt, gas moves downward easily, the senses feel clear and the body feels light. Eating at that point lets the digestive fire do its job. Eating before the previous meal has cleared, or when upset, rushed or not truly hungry, invites indigestion. So let real hunger and these body signals, not the clock or the company, decide when to eat.","chapter":8},{"problem":"After-meal drinks (Anupana) — what to drink with food","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"combination","guidance":"A good after-drink has qualities opposite to the meal but not clashing with it; it helps food move, soften and digest, and leaves you satisfied. Cold water suits meals of wheat and barley and follows curd, honey or wine. Warm water suits starchy, heavy, legume and buttermilk-based meals. For practical body types, honey water suits the overweight, meat soup or nourishing drinks suit the thin and depleted, and milk acts like a tonic for those weakened by illness, long travel, speaking, fasting or sun, and for the old and young. But skip heavy after-drinks if you have throat, nose, ear or eye complaints, cough, breathing trouble or hoarseness. A","chapter":8},{"problem":"How a substance acts: taste, quality, potency, post-digestive effect and special effect","category":"General","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Ayurveda explains the action of any food or medicine through five layers. Rasa is the taste you perceive (sweet, sour, salty, pungent, bitter, astringent). Guna is its quality (heavy or light, oily or dry, hot or cold, and so on). Virya is its potency, mainly whether it is heating or cooling. Vipaka is the taste it turns into after digestion. Prabhava is a special, unique action that cannot be explained by the other four. When these point in different directions, the strongest one decides the effect, and there is a natural order: potency (Virya) outranks taste and Vipaka, and special effect (Prabhava) outranks everything. Knowing these layers","chapter":9},{"problem":"Hot versus cold potency (Virya) of foods and herbs","category":"Energy","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Potency (Virya) is the single most practical property, and the common view reduces it to two, heating (Ushna) and cooling (Sheeta), because fire and water are the two great forces of nature. Heating substances speed up digestion and transformation, can cause thirst, sweating and a burning feeling if overused, and they calm Vata and Kapha. Cooling substances soothe, refresh and steady the body, settle Pitta and calm the blood. In daily use this means: if you run hot, are acidic or Pitta-aggravated, favour cooling foods; if you are cold, sluggish or Kapha- or Vata-aggravated, favour warming foods and spices. Judging a food by its heating or coo","chapter":9},{"problem":"Post-digestive effect (Vipaka) — what food becomes after digestion","category":"Digestion","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Vipaka is the taste a food transforms into at the end of digestion, and it often matters more than the taste on your tongue. The rule is simple: sweet and salty foods become sweet after digestion (nourishing, building, grounding), sour foods stay sour, and bitter, pungent and astringent foods become pungent (lightening, drying, stimulating). This is why a food can taste one way yet have a different long-term effect on the body. When you want to know whether a food will ultimately nourish and build you or lighten and dry you, look to its Vipaka, not just its immediate taste.","chapter":9},{"problem":"Special effect (Prabhava) and why similar foods can act differently","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"Sometimes two substances share the same taste, qualities, potency and post-digestive effect, yet one does something the other simply does not. That unexplained, unique action is called Prabhava, a special effect that overrides all the ordinary properties. Classic examples: Danti purges while the similar Chitraka does not; dry grapes gently purge while licorice does not; ghee boosts digestive strength while milk, otherwise similar, does not. The lesson is that general categories only take you so far. You must know a substance's actual, observed behaviour, because its signature effect can surprise you even when everything on paper looks the sam","chapter":9},{"problem":"The five elements and the nature of every substance","category":"General","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Ayurveda teaches that everything, food, herb or medicine, is built from five great elements in different proportions: Earth, Water, Fire, Air and Ether. Whichever element dominates gives the substance its nature. Earth-dominant things are heavy, solid and nourishing; water-dominant things are liquid, cooling, moist and binding; fire-dominant things are hot, sharp and digestive; air-dominant things are light, dry and mobile; ether-dominant things are subtle and light. Fire- and air-rich substances tend to move energy upward in the body, while earth- and water-rich ones move it downward. This framework is the reason the same six tastes can prod","chapter":9},{"problem":"Nothing is without medicinal use","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"A central teaching of this chapter is that there is no substance in the world that cannot serve as medicine; what decides whether it helps or harms is knowing the substance well and using it for the right purpose, in the right way and amount. The same item can be food, poison or medicine depending on the person, the dose and the context. The practical message is humility and precision: do not divide things rigidly into good and bad, but learn each substance's taste, potency and effect, and apply it thoughtfully to the right condition.","chapter":9},{"problem":"Sweet taste (Madhura) - effects and when it harms","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Sweet taste is formed from the earth and water elements. It is the taste we know from birth and the most nourishing of all. It builds every body tissue and increases strength, which is why it suits children, the elderly, the injured and the weak. It improves the skin, hair, the sense organs and ojas, the vital essence tied to immunity. It firms up the body, soothes the throat, increases breast milk and helps broken bones mend. Being oily and slow to digest, it promotes long life. In the doshas it calms both Pitta and Vata and helps counter poison. Its danger lies in excess: too much sweet food builds Kapha and fat, leading to obesity, breathi","chapter":10},{"problem":"Sour taste (Amla) - effects and when it harms","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Sour taste comes from the fire and water elements. It kindles the digestive fire, pleases the heart and sharpens the appetite. It is oily, hot in its internal potency yet cooling when applied to the skin, where it relieves burning. It moistens the body, is light and easy to digest, and it nudges stuck Vata to move downward in its natural direction. In the doshas it raises Kapha, Pitta and the blood. Used in excess it slackens the body and drains strength, and can bring dimness of vision, giddiness, itching, a pale anaemic look, spreading herpes-type skin disease, swellings, pox-like eruptions, thirst and fevers. Note the general rule that sou","chapter":10},{"problem":"Salt taste (Lavana) - effects and when it harms","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Salt taste is formed from the water and fire elements. It loosens stiffness and rigidity, clears blockages in the body's channels and pores, strengthens digestion, lubricates the tissues, encourages sweating and penetrates deep into the body. It improves the flavour of food and helps abscesses open and drain. In balance it is useful, but in excess it spoils the blood and stirs up Vata, which can show as baldness, premature greying, wrinkled skin, thirst, skin diseases, poison-like effects, spreading skin disease and an overall loss of bodily strength. A classical note adds that salts are generally unfriendly to the eyes, with rock salt as the","chapter":10},{"problem":"Bitter taste (Tikta) - effects and when it harms","category":"Diet & Food","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Bitter taste arises from the ether and air elements. Though it is not pleasant by itself, it is genuinely corrective: it helps with loss of appetite, worms, thirst, the effects of poison, skin diseases, fainting, fever, nausea and burning sensations. In the doshas it calms both Pitta and Kapha. It dries up excess moisture, fat, muscle-fat, marrow, stool and urine, so it is strongly drying. It is light and easy to digest, cool in potency, sharpens intelligence and helps cleanse breast milk and the throat. Its weakness is that in excess it thins and depletes the body tissues and tends to provoke Vata disorders, which is why very bitter, very dr","chapter":10},{"problem":"Pungent taste (Katu) - effects and when it harms","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Pungent taste is formed from the fire and air elements. It clears throat ailments, allergic rashes, skin diseases, a form of indigestion called alasaka and various swellings, and it reduces the swelling around ulcers. It dries up oiliness, fat and excess water, increases hunger, aids digestion, improves taste, cleanses the system and helps expel aggravated doshas, dries the moisture in food, breaks up hard lumps and opens the body's channels. In the doshas it reduces Kapha. Overused, it causes thirst, depletion of semen and strength, fainting, muscle cramps, tremors and pain in the waist and back. The text also notes that bitter and pungent g","chapter":10},{"problem":"Astringent taste (Kashaya) - effects and when it harms","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Astringent taste comes from the earth and air elements. It settles both Pitta and Kapha. It is slow to digest, cleanses the blood, presses and helps heal wounds, is cold in potency, and dries up moisture and fat. It tends to slow the digestion of food still undigested. Because it absorbs water, it can firm the stool to the point of constipation and dryness, and it can over-cleanse the skin. In excess it keeps food stuck and undigested, causing bloating, pain in the chest region, thirst, wasting, loss of virility, blocked channels and constipation. A general rule notes astringents are usually cold and obstructive, with haritaki as the exceptio","chapter":10},{"problem":"How tastes behave - potency, dryness and weight","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"Besides their direct effects, the six tastes also behave in predictable ways that guide how they are used. In terms of heating and cooling, pungent, sour and salt are heating in potency, each stronger than the one before, while bitter, astringent and sweet are cooling, again in increasing order. In terms of dryness, bitter, pungent and astringent are drying and tend to constipate, whereas salt, sour and sweet are oily and help the body pass stool, urine and gas. In terms of weight, salt, astringent and sweet are heavy and slow to digest, while sour, pungent and bitter are light and digest easily. The tastes can be used alone or blended, givin","chapter":10},{"problem":"Qualities and functions of Vata, and what raises it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Vata is the principle of all movement. When it is healthy it drives the flow of blood and air, moves the limbs, gives enthusiasm, runs breathing, controls the mind and speech, starts off natural urges like tears, stool, urine, sneezing and yawning, keeps the tissues in order and keeps the senses alert. Its home tissue is the bone, and unusually it relates to bone in reverse: when Vata rises, bone wastes, and when Vata falls, bone builds up. Vata increases mainly with under-nutrition, dryness, cold and too much movement or activity, and decreases with over-nutrition and nourishment. For this reason classical teaching treats raised Vata with bu","chapter":11},{"problem":"Qualities and functions of Pitta, and what raises it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Pitta is the principle of digestion, heat and transformation. When healthy it runs digestion and metabolism, holds body temperature steady, powers vision, creates hunger, thirst and appetite, and maintains skin colour, intelligence, courage and bodily softness. Its home is in sweat and blood, and it moves with them: when Pitta rises, the spoiling of sweat and blood rises too, and the same in reverse. Pitta tends to increase with over-nutrition that later shows as raised Kapha in general terms, but Pitta specifically is stirred by heat and sharp, sour, salty influences. Conditions from excess Pitta are treated with lightening, cooling (Langhan","chapter":11},{"problem":"Qualities and functions of Kapha, and what raises it","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Kapha is the principle of structure, stability and lubrication. When healthy it gives the body firmness, lubricates and binds the joints, and supports mental steadiness, patience and the capacity to hold back emotion and strain. Its home is the remaining tissues and the wastes, and it moves with them in the usual direction, rising and falling together. Kapha increases mainly with over-nutrition, which is why excess tends to appear as the heavy, cold, sluggish signs above. Conditions from excess Kapha are treated with lightening, reducing (Langhana) measures that cut heaviness and moisture, while deficiency is built back with nourishing measur","chapter":11},{"problem":"The seven tissues (dhatus) - roles and signs of excess or deficiency","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"The body has seven tissues, each with its own job. Rasa (plasma) nourishes; rakta (blood) sustains life activity; mamsa (muscle) covers and supports and enables movement; meda (fat) lubricates; asthi (bone) gives the frame; majja (marrow) fills the bones; shukra (reproductive fluid) enables conception. When a tissue increases it produces more of its own nature: excess blood brings spreading skin disease, bleeding disorders, jaundice and redness; excess muscle enlarges cheeks, thighs and abdomen; excess fat brings breathlessness and drooping; excess bone overgrows bone and teeth; excess marrow brings heaviness and stubborn ulcers; excess repro","chapter":11},{"problem":"The three wastes (malas) - roles and signs of excess or deficiency","category":"Digestion","dosha":"—","guidance":"The body has three main wastes, each with a healthy purpose. Faeces help maintain the body's bulk and strength; urine carries off liquid waste; sweat slowly carries off moisture. When they increase, the signs reflect too much of each: a distended, noisy, heavy abdomen from faeces, severe bladder pain and a sense of not having emptied from urine, and heavy, foul, itchy perspiration from sweat. When they decrease, the little that forms is hard to perceive and must be inferred from dryness, pricking pain, emptiness and tightness at the sites where waste is made and released. Because the body is used to carrying a normal load of waste in the gut ","chapter":11},{"problem":"How to tell which dosha, tissue or waste is out of balance","category":"General","dosha":"—","guidance":"Ayurveda reads imbalance by qualities, not just quantity. A dosha, tissue or waste that has increased reveals itself by showing more of its own natural qualities, while one that has decreased shows the opposite qualities. Wastes are judged increased when they build up and fail to clear, causing obstruction, and decreased when only small amounts pass. Increase of doshas, tissues and wastes generally traces back to over-nutrition, which later presents as raised Kapha, while decrease traces back to under-nutrition, which later presents as raised Vata. This guides treatment: excess is reduced with lightening (Langhana) measures and deficiency is ","chapter":11},{"problem":"Ojas - the vital essence, its loss and increase","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"Ojas is the subtle essence of all seven tissues, the most refined product of shukra. It sits mainly in the heart but pervades the whole body and is the basis of health; where ojas is, life is, and its loss is the loss of life. It is described as oily, watery, clear and slightly reddish-yellow. Ojas is depleted by anger, starvation, worry, grief and overexertion, and classical texts add overexercise, long fasting, under-eating, drying drinks, poor food combinations, fear, staying awake at night, injury, infection, tissue loss such as from bleeding, excessive loss of Kapha, blood, semen or waste, and poison. When ojas falls the person becomes f","chapter":11},{"problem":"Prana Vata - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Prana is the first of the five sub-types of Vata. It is seated in the head and moves through the chest and throat. It governs the higher functions: the will, the heart, the sense organs, the intellect and vision. On the physical side it drives expectoration, sneezing, belching, the drawing in of the breath and the swallowing of food. Because it rules breathing and the mind, it is the most vital of the Vata sub-types. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Udana Vata - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Udana is the second sub-type of Vata. Its seat is the chest, and it moves through the nose, navel and throat. It is the upward-moving air behind expression and vitality: it starts off speech, powers effort and enthusiasm, and maintains strength, colour and complexion and memory. In simple terms, it is the force behind how a person speaks, exerts themselves and holds their vigour. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Vyana Vata - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Vyana is the third sub-type of Vata. It is seated in the heart and races throughout the entire body at great speed. It carries out all forms of movement: bending and straightening the limbs, the opening and closing of the eyelids, and generally every bodily action. Because it pervades everywhere so quickly, nearly all the body's activities are regulated by Vyana. This is traditional teaching offered for learning, not a prescription.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Samana Vata - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Samana is the fourth sub-type of Vata. It sits beside the digestive fire and moves through the kostha, the alimentary tract. Its job is tied to digestion: it receives food into the stomach, helps cook and digest it, separates the nourishing part from the waste part, and then moves each of these along its proper path. In effect it is the air that keeps the digestive process orderly. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Apana Vata - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Apana is the fifth sub-type of Vata. It is seated in the large intestine and moves through the waist, bladder and genitals. It is the downward-moving air that handles elimination and reproduction: ejaculation, menstruation, defecation, urination and childbirth. Because it governs the natural downward urges, its balance matters for regular elimination and reproductive health. Shared for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Pachaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Pachaka is the first of the five sub-types of Pitta. It sits between the stomach and the large intestine. Though made of all five elements, it is dominant in the fire element and lacks the water element, which is why it is called anala, fire. Its work is digestion: it cooks the food and divides it into essence and waste. It also lends its grace and power to the other four Pittas, supporting their functions. For this reason Pachaka is considered the chief among the Pittas. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Ranjaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Ranjaka is the second sub-type of Pitta. It is seated in the stomach. Its name comes from colouring, because its job is to colour and convert the useful product of digestion into blood. In the language of the tissues, it transforms rasa (plasma) into rakta (blood). It is therefore central to the formation and quality of the blood. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Sadhaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Sadhaka is the third sub-type of Pitta. It is seated in the heart. Unlike the digestive Pittas, its work is mental: it supports knowledge, intelligence and self-awareness, and in doing so helps a person accomplish the aims and purposes of life. It is, in a sense, the Pitta behind clear thinking and achievement. This is traditional teaching offered for learning, not a prescription.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Alochaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Alochaka is the fourth sub-type of Pitta. It is seated in the eyes, and its single clear function is to enable vision, the taking in and processing of what is seen. Of the five Pittas it is the one dedicated to sight. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Bhrajaka Pitta - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Bhrajaka is the fifth sub-type of Pitta. It resides in the skin, and its job is to bring out colour and complexion, giving the skin its glow and appearance. It is the Pitta that governs how the skin looks and how it displays colour. Shared for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Avalambaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Avalambaka is the first of the five sub-types of Kapha. It is seated in the chest and at the trika, the meeting place of the shoulders, neck and back. By its own innate strength and by the power drawn from the essence of food, it carries out the watery functions of lubrication and nourishment and provides support. It also influences and supports the other four Kaphas, which is why it is considered the foremost. This is educational background from a classical text, not a treatment plan.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Kledaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Digestion","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Kledaka is the second sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the stomach. Its job is to moisten and soften the hard mass of food so that digestion can proceed smoothly. It is the Kapha that keeps the digestive contents suitably moist. Shared for understanding only, not as medical advice.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Bodhaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Bodhaka is the third sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the tongue, and its function is to enable the perception of taste. Its name comes from this awakening of taste awareness, so it is the Kapha that lets us register flavour. This is traditional teaching offered for learning, not a prescription.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Tarpaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Tarpaka is the fourth sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the head, and its job is to nourish and satisfy the sense organs, keeping them well supported. It is the Kapha that sustains the senses from its seat in the head. Educational information from an ancient source, not a cure.","chapter":12},{"problem":"Shleshaka Kapha - seat and role","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Shleshaka is the fifth sub-type of Kapha. It is seated in the bone joints. Its function is to lubricate and strengthen the joints, keeping them smooth and stable. Its name comes from binding, as it holds the joints together. Shared for learning, not as treatment.","chapter":12},{"problem":"How doshas move and the stages of imbalance (Chaya, Kopa, Shama) across the seasons","category":"Prevention","dosha":"combination","guidance":"A dosha does not go from balanced to diseased in one step; it passes through three stages. Chaya is a mild build-up in the dosha's own home, during which the body protects itself by creating a dislike for whatever would raise that dosha more. Kopa or prakopa is the overflow, when the raised dosha spills from its seat into other channels, early warning symptoms appear and disease can start. Shama is the settling back to normal, the return of health. The qualities that drive each dosha differ: Vata builds with hot-dry, overflows with cold-dry and settles with warm-oily; Pitta builds with cold-sharp, overflows with hot-sharp and settles with col","chapter":12},{"problem":"How to balance aggravated Vata","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Treat raised Vata with warmth, oiliness, grounding and comfort. Use oleation (sneha): oil or ghee taken internally as advised, plus regular warm oil self-massage (abhyanga) and simple rubbing. Follow massage with sudation or gentle sweating (sveda), and warm herbal pouring (seka) or wrapping the affected part in cloth for warmth. Favour foods that are sweet, sour and salty in taste, served warm and moist, and warming medicated fats prepared with appetite-kindling, digestion-improving herbs. Oily, warming enemas (basti, including anuvasana oleation enema from fatty meat juice and oil) are a classic Vata measure. Keep life calm, warm and reassu","chapter":13},{"problem":"How to calm excess Pitta","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Cool, soothe and gently clear Pitta. Favour foods and herbs that are sweet, bitter and astringent, and cooling in nature; milk and ghee are specifically useful. Ghee may be taken (plain or medicated) on a physician's advice. Anoint the body often with cooling pastes of sandalwood, camphor and vetiver, and inhale cool, pleasant herbal fragrances. Build a calm, cooling lifestyle: moonlit evenings, soft breeze, gentle music, time near clean water, gardens and fountains, and the company of easy, agreeable people, keeping the mind peaceful. When clearing is needed, purgation (Virechana) using sweet-tasting, cooling herbs is the preferred route for","chapter":13},{"problem":"How to reduce excess Kapha","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Lighten, dry, warm and stimulate to bring Kapha down. Eat dry, light foods in small amounts and favour tastes that are pungent, bitter and astringent, along with sharp and warming qualities; grain soups and honey are helpful, and fat-reducing herbs and therapies are indicated. Lifestyle should be active and even a little demanding: varied exercise, dry massage (udvartana), staying awake rather than oversleeping, and accepting some effort and hardship. Inhaling medicinal smoke, gargling and periods of fasting all reduce Kapha. Strong, properly conducted emesis (Vamana) and purgation are the classic deep-clearing therapies for Kapha but, being ","chapter":13},{"problem":"How to treat two or three doshas aggravated together","category":"Dosha Balance","dosha":"combination","guidance":"When doshas combine, blend their individual remedies sensibly rather than treating only one. A traditional seasonal guide: manage Vata-with-Pitta as in the summer regimen, Kapha-with-Vata as in the spring regimen (because Vata is a carrier that amplifies whichever dosha it joins), and Kapha-with-Pitta as in the autumn regimen. Act early, while a dosha is only mildly accumulating, if you can. Once a dosha is truly aggravated, reduce it with measures that do not stir up the others. If all three flare at once, control the strongest or most aggravated one first, still choosing measures that do not worsen the rest. A sound treatment relieves the p","chapter":13},{"problem":"When doshas are mixed with Ama (Sama) and how to clear them safely","category":"Detox","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Ama forms when weak digestion leaves nutrient fluid (Rasa) raw and spoiled in the gut; doshas tainted by it become sticky and spread into the tissues. Do not force these out with strong cleansing, which is compared to squeezing an unripe fruit and only damages the tissues. Follow the order: first use digestive and appetite-kindling herbs to 'ripen' the Ama; then oleation and sudation (oiling and sweating); and only when ready, clear the doshas by purification at the right season and to the person's strength. Medicine by mouth clears the stomach and small intestine, by nose the head and neck region, by rectum the large intestine. If the body i","chapter":13},{"problem":"The right season and time of day to clear doshas and take medicine","category":"Prevention","dosha":"—","guidance":"Doshas that quietly build up in one season are best cleared in the following mild transition: Vata (built up in summer) by enema around Shravana (about August), Pitta (built up in the rains) by purgation in Kartika, and Kapha (built up in winter) by emesis around Chaitra (about April). First protect the body from the harsh heat, rain or cold of each season, then give the therapy, and do not let the stages of a developing illness advance unchecked. For daily timing, match the medicine to the condition: empty stomach for strong Kapha problems and strong people; just before food for lower-Vata (Apana) issues; mid-meal for Samana Vata; end of mea","chapter":13},{"problem":"Reducing therapy (Langhana) — when and how","category":"Detox","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Langhana (also called Apatarpana) lightens, dries and clears the body, and is the right direction when there is excess, heaviness or blockage. It has two broad forms. Shamana (palliative) brings an abnormal dosha back to normal without expelling it, through seven measures: digestive/carminative herbs (Pachana), appetite-kindling measures (Deepana), bearing hunger or light fasting (Kshut), reducing fluids and bearing thirth (Trut), exercise (Vyayama), sun exposure (Aatapa) and exposure to breeze (Maruta). Shodhana (purification) forcibly expels aggravated doshas through five clinical procedures: emesis, purgation, decoction enema, nasal cleans","chapter":14},{"problem":"Nourishing therapy (Brimhana) — when and how","category":"Energy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"Brimhana (also called Santarpana) nourishes, enriches and rebuilds the body, and is the right direction when there is depletion, dryness or weakness. It works mainly through the heavy, moist earth and water qualities, and also calms Vata and Vata-Pitta, so it is itself a gentle balancing (Shamana) therapy. The classic measures are rich and grounding: meat, milk, sugar, ghee and healthy fats; nourishing enemas made with sweet substances and oils; good sleep and a comfortable bed; oil massage and bathing; and above all rest, comfort and a calm, happy mind, free of worry. For a lean person, light but nourishing foods such as wheat suit better th","chapter":14},{"problem":"Signs you have been over-treated or under-treated","category":"General","dosha":"combination","guidance":"Both therapies harm when pushed past what is needed, so dose and measure are the key. Over-nourishing (Ati-Brimhana) shows as profound obesity and may bring scrofula, diabetes and urinary disease, fever, abdominal swelling, fistula, cough, loss of consciousness, painful urination, Ama disorders and serious skin disease; it is corrected by fat-, Vata- and Kapha-reducing measures such as horse gram, barley, green gram, millets and honey-water, more activity, less sleep, purification, and daily licks of Triphala, Guduchi, chebulic myrobalan or nutgrass with honey, along with classical fat-reducing herbal formulations. Over-reducing (Ati-Langhana","chapter":14},{"problem":"Groups of cleansing herbs (Shodhanadi Gana) — what they are","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"This is a classical catalogue that sorts dozens of plants and minerals into named families, each sharing one action, so a physician can reach for a whole group and substitute within it. It underpins how Ayurvedic medicines are formulated, but it is reference material for a trained practitioner who dispenses medicine, not a home remedy. Many listed plants are potent or toxic and must be correctly identified and processed. Selecting, combining and dosing them is specialist work done only by a qualified Ayurvedic physician after assessing the individual. Never gather, mix or take these substances at home, and never let them replace prescribed me","chapter":15},{"problem":"Emetic group (Chardana Gana) — herbs for therapeutic vomiting","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"This family, led by Madana fruit along with liquorice, neem and Kutaja, provides the drugs classically used to bring on controlled therapeutic vomiting as part of cleansing therapy. Choosing and preparing an emetic drug, judging its strength and matching it to the patient are entirely the work of a trained Ayurvedic physician in a clinical setting. Therapeutic emesis is a medical procedure with real risks and is never something to attempt at home or improvise from these plants. It must never replace treatment your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Purgative group (Virechana Gana) — herbs for downward cleansing","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"This group gathers the downward-acting cleansing agents such as Trivrit, Triphala and Snuhi latex that classical texts use for therapeutic purgation. The strength of these drugs varies enormously and some are harsh, so the selection, processing and dosing belong strictly to a qualified Ayurvedic physician who has assessed the patient. Purgation therapy is a clinical procedure, not a home detox, and these herbs should never be gathered or taken on one's own. It is not a substitute for prescribed medical care.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Dosha-balancing groups (Vataghna, Pittaghna, Kaphaghna Gana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata, Pitta or Kapha depending on the group","guidance":"These three families each settle one dosha: one calms Vata, one cools Pitta, and one reduces Kapha. A physician draws from the right family when building a formula for a particular imbalance. Deciding which dosha is actually disturbed, and in what proportion, requires formal clinical assessment, so matching herbs to a person is the physician's task, not a self-diagnosis exercise. These groups are formulation references for trained practitioners and are not meant for home preparation or self-treatment, and they do not replace prescribed medicine.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Restorative and nourishing groups (Jeevaniya, Vidaryadi, Padmakadi Gana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata and Pitta (nourishing)","guidance":"Alongside the cleansing families, the chapter lists nourishing groups meant to rebuild strength, tissue and vitality, support the heart, and in some cases promote breast milk. These are often used after cleansing therapy or in states of depletion. Even restorative herbs must be chosen and prepared correctly for the individual, which is a physician's judgement. They are formulation references for a trained practitioner, not self-prescribed tonics, and they should not be taken at home in place of proper medical advice or prescribed treatment.","chapter":15},{"problem":"Symptom-targeted and substitution groups (Sarivadi, Patoladi, Varunadi and others)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"Most remaining families are organised around a symptom picture, covering complaints like fever, skin disease, bleeding tendencies, thirst, urinary problems, abdominal masses and chronic wounds. The chapter also allows a physician to substitute an unavailable herb with one of the same properties and to work these drugs into prepared forms such as pastes, decoctions, medicated fats and confections. All of this is formulation knowledge for a trained Ayurvedic physician who evaluates the patient first. It is not a guide to self-treatment, these potent plants should never be gathered or combined at home, and nothing here replaces prescribed medica","chapter":15},{"problem":"Oleation therapy (Snehana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata (and prepares the body for cleansing)","guidance":"Oleation means giving carefully measured medicinal fats so the body becomes lubricated inside and out, which softens and loosens stuck waste before deeper cleansing is done, and can also calm an aggravated dosha on its own. This is a supervised medical procedure, not the ordinary use of ghee in cooking: the dose is fixed to the person's digestion, often with a small test dose first, and the person is watched daily. Choosing the fat, setting the dose and running the course are all tasks for a qualified Ayurvedic physician after proper assessment. It must never be attempted at home and never replaces prescribed medical care.","chapter":16},{"problem":"Choosing and matching the fats (ghee, oil, muscle fat, marrow)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"Classical texts use four fats, ghee, oil, muscle fat and marrow, and the physician picks and sometimes combines them to fit the patient, since each behaves differently and digests at a different rate. Ghee is generally favoured for calming Pitta and supporting the mind, oil for Kapha and fat disorders, and the heavier fats for depleted tissue and specific pains. Judging which fat suits a given person is a clinical decision requiring assessment and training. This is physician's knowledge for preparing therapy, not a basis for self-dosing fats at home, and it does not replace prescribed treatment.","chapter":16},{"problem":"Who should and should not receive oleation","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"The texts carefully separate those who benefit from oleation from those who would be harmed by it, and the list of people to avoid is long, including those with very weak or very strong digestion, the obese, diarrhoea, indigestion, throat disease, chronic alcoholism and women after miscarriage. Working out whether a specific person is a safe candidate requires a physician's examination, because getting it wrong can cause real harm. This screening is a clinical judgement, never a self-assessment, and the therapy itself must not be tried at home or used in place of care your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":16},{"problem":"Reading the signs and managing complications of oleation","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"A physician judges the result daily by clear signs: proper oleation brings keen digestion, soft stools, downward movement of Vata and a natural aversion to fat, while pallor and oily secretions mean it has been overdone and other signs mean the body is still too dry. Wrong dose, fat, timing or diet can bring complications such as swelling, piles, stupor, itching, fever, nausea and bloating, which the physician treats with fasting, lighter drying foods and specific remedies. Interpreting these signs and handling problems needs training and supervision. None of this is safe to manage at home, and it is not a replacement for prescribed medical c","chapter":16},{"problem":"Sweating therapy (Swedana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha and Vata","guidance":"Sweating therapy uses controlled heat to make the body sweat on purpose, normally after oleation, so that softened waste is melted and moved toward the digestive tract for removal, while also easing cold, stiffness and pain. The heat, method, duration and intensity are all set by a qualified Ayurvedic physician for the individual, and the procedure is done in a prepared, supervised setting. This is a clinical treatment, not a home steam or sauna session, and it must never be attempted alone or used in place of prescribed medical care.","chapter":17},{"problem":"The four methods of sweating (dry heat, poultice, steam, warm liquid)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha and Vata","guidance":"Classical texts describe four approaches, direct dry heat, warm medicated poultices, directed steam, and pouring warm medicated liquid, and the physician chooses among them to suit the disease and the part of the body being treated, often adjusting whether fats are included based on the dosha. Each method involves real heat and medicated materials that must be prepared and applied correctly. Selecting and carrying out these methods is specialist clinical work. They are not home procedures, and using them should never replace the care your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":17},{"problem":"Who should not be sweated, and protecting delicate areas","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"The texts carefully list the many people who must not be sweated, or only very mildly, including the very weak, dehydrated, bleeding, those with indigestion or Pitta disorders, and women who are pregnant, menstruating or recently delivered, and they insist that delicate areas like the eyes, heart region and groin receive very little or no heat. Judging whether a person is a safe candidate, and protecting vulnerable organs, requires a physician's examination and skill. This screening cannot be done at home, the therapy is not for self-use, and it does not replace prescribed medical care.","chapter":17},{"problem":"Reading proper sweating and managing excess","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"A physician judges success by the easing of cold, pain and stiffness and the softening of the part, after which gentle massage and a warm bath follow. Over-sweating is harmful and can cause thirst, fainting, weakness, dizziness, joint pain, fever and skin discoloration, which the physician then checks with cooling, holding measures that must themselves be balanced carefully. Recognising when sweating is enough, and treating excess safely, needs training and continuous supervision. None of this is safe to manage at home, and it is not a substitute for prescribed medical treatment.","chapter":17},{"problem":"Therapeutic emesis (Vamana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"Vamana is controlled therapeutic vomiting used to expel excess Kapha after the body has been prepared with oleation and sweating. In classical practice the physician prepares the patient over days, gives a measured emetic medicine, supervises the bouts, and judges by their number and nature whether the cleansing was too little, correct, or excessive, the last of which is dangerous. This is a high-risk clinical procedure with a long list of people it must not be given to, and over-treatment can even be fatal. It is carried out only by a qualified Ayurvedic physician in a supervised setting, must never be attempted at home or self-dosed, and mu","chapter":18},{"problem":"Therapeutic purgation (Virechana) — what it is","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Pitta","guidance":"Virechana is controlled therapeutic purgation used mainly to expel excess Pitta, again after the body is prepared with oleation and sweating. The physician matches the drug and dose to the person's bowel nature and dosha, gives it at the proper time, and reads the result by the bouts and material passed, watching closely for over-purgation. The texts warn that a large dose given once can risk death and that weak or unprepared patients need mild, small, repeated doses or other precautions. This is a serious clinical procedure done only by a trained Ayurvedic physician with the patient under supervision. It must never be attempted at home, neve","chapter":18},{"problem":"Screening and preparation before emesis or purgation","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"Before either procedure, the physician decides whether the person is a safe candidate using detailed classical criteria, and prepares the body with oleation and sweating so the waste is loosened and can be expelled without harm. The texts warn that purifying a body that has not first been properly prepared can injure the person like bending a dry stick. This screening and preparation is careful clinical judgement based on examining the individual. It cannot be done at home or self-assessed, and it is never a substitute for the care your doctor has prescribed.","chapter":18},{"problem":"Reading the outcome and the recovery diet (Samsarjana Karma)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"The physician judges whether cleansing was inadequate, proper or excessive by the number and nature of the bouts and the material passed, and treats over-treatment promptly, since it can cause severe weakness, dehydration and other dangers. Because both procedures leave the digestive fire weak, recovery runs through a careful graded diet, from thin gruel up to normal food over a set number of meals, which is itself part of the medical plan. Interpreting the outcome, handling complications and guiding recovery all require a trained Ayurvedic physician's supervision. This is not something to manage at home, and it does not replace prescribed me","chapter":18},{"problem":"Decoction enema (Niruha / Asthapana Basti)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"This is a cleansing enema in which a warm herbal decoction, blended with salt, honey, fat and a little herbal paste, is introduced through the rectum to draw out accumulated wastes and pacify disordered Vata. It is given on an empty stomach, after prior oleation and sweating, with the quantity matched to the person's age and strength. It is classically avoided in the very weak, in chest injury, recent or toxin-type diarrhoea, vomiting, breathlessness, piles, ascites, diabetes and in pregnancy. Because the fluid must return within a short, defined window and the dosing and timing demand close judgement, this is a formal clinical procedure carr","chapter":19},{"problem":"Oil enema (Anuvasana / Sneha Basti)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"This is a nourishing, lubricating enema using medicated oil or ghee introduced through the rectum to soften and calm Vata and ease the channels. It is classically given soon after a light meal, in a smaller quantity than the decoction enema, and is usually alternated with decoction enema so the body is both lubricated and cleansed rather than one alone. The very low-dose version, Matra Basti, is described as gentle and well tolerated for children, the elderly, the overworked and the weak. Suitability, dose, timing and aftercare are decided case by case, so this is performed only by or under a trained Ayurvedic physician. It is not to be self-","chapter":19},{"problem":"Urethral, vaginal and uterine enema (Uttarabasti)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"This is a specialised enema delivered through the urethra, or in women through the vaginal and uterine route, using a fine smooth nozzle and a very small, mild quantity of medicated fat. In women it is classically timed to the menstrual period when the passage is open, and given in short courses. Because it involves delicate internal passages and tiny precise doses, it carries real risk if done wrongly and is among the most technical procedures in the text. It is performed strictly by a trained Ayurvedic physician in a clinical setting, is never to be attempted at home, and is not a replacement for medical evaluation and care.","chapter":19},{"problem":"Cleansing nasal therapy (Virechana / Shodhana Nasya)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha","guidance":"This is a purifying nasal therapy in which medicated fats, pastes, decoctions or strong juices are instilled or blown into the nostrils to draw stuck Doshas out of the head and neck. The classical method involves prior oil massage and mild sweating of the head, lying with the face up, warming the medicine, instilling it into each nostril in turn, then spitting out what drains. It is strongly dose- and timing-dependent and is classically withheld from the recently fed, the recently bathed, after alcohol or blood-letting, in acute rhinitis, cough or breathlessness, in new mothers and outside set age limits. Because the purgative and powder form","chapter":20},{"problem":"Nourishing nasal therapy (Brimhana Nasya)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"This is a strengthening nasal therapy using rich, nourishing materials such as meat juice or blood of suitable animals combined with resins and plant extracts, instilled into the nostrils to relieve dryness and Vata disorders of the head. It follows the same careful preparation as other nasal therapy, with head massage and mild fomentation, correct posture, warming of the medicine and drop-counted dosing. Suitability, material and dose are judged by the physician against the person and the season. For these reasons it is carried out only by or under a qualified Ayurvedic physician, is not something to self-administer, and is not a substitute ","chapter":20},{"problem":"Low-dose daily nasal oil (Pratimarsha Nasya)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"This is the very low-dose form of oily nasal therapy, just a drop or two of medicated oil, which the classical text presents as mild enough for regular use from childhood to old age and at many everyday moments, such as after a head massage, gargle, tooth cleaning or bowel movement. It is said to cleanse and clear the nasal channels, ease fatigue, help eyesight, firm the teeth and settle Vata, and to need far fewer restrictions than the stronger dose. Even so, the choice of oil, such as the classical Anu Taila, and whether it suits a given person are decided by a qualified Ayurvedic physician, and it is best begun under that guidance rather t","chapter":20},{"problem":"Medicated smoke inhalation (Dhumapana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Kapha and Vata","guidance":"This therapy draws the smoke of specific medicinal herbs through a purpose-made multi-chambered pipe to act on the head, nose, throat and chest, mainly against Kapha and Vata. The smoke comes in graded strengths, soft for Vata, medium for mixed Vata and Kapha, and strong for Kapha, and the classical rules fix exactly when each may be used and how many measured breaths are taken, with the smoke always let out through the mouth and never the nose. The text warns at length against it in bleeding disorders, after purgation or enema, in certain eye disease, head injury, anaemia and many other states, and says misuse or wrong timing can cause bleed","chapter":21},{"problem":"Daily oil pulling and gentle mouth rinse (simple Gandusha / Kavala)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"In its gentlest everyday form this means holding or lightly swishing a mild liquid in the mouth. The classical text treats plain comfortably warm water, daily sesame oil holding, and simple rinses as ordinary daily care that cleanses the mouth, freshens the breath, and comforts the teeth and gums. Used this way, gentle warm-water or mild oil-pulling practice can reasonably be a soft self-care habit for most healthy adults. It should stay gentle and non-medicated: anything using strong, hot, pungent or purifying herbal preparations, or done to treat an actual mouth or throat disease, moves out of self-care and should be set up and supervised b","chapter":22},{"problem":"Medicated mouth hold and gargle (Gandusha / Kavala, strong types)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata, Pitta or Kapha by type","guidance":"In its therapeutic form, medicine is held full in the mouth and kept still, or taken in smaller amount and gargled, using liquids matched to the disorder: oils for Vata, bitter and astringent and sweet herbs for Pitta, hot pungent and salty herbs for Kapha, and astringent bitter herbs to heal. The mouth is prepared with shoulder and neck massage and fomentation, the liquid held until the mouth fills or the eyes and nose run, and the practice is classically avoided in indigestion, locked jaw, loss of sleep and certain other states. Because the choice of strong or medicated liquid and the judgement of timing matter, these medicated forms are se","chapter":22},{"problem":"Oil therapy of the head (Murdha Taila: Shirodhara, Shiro Basti and related)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Vata","guidance":"This is a group of head-oil therapies of rising strength: oiling with gentle massage, pouring warm medicated oil in a steady stream over the head (Shirodhara), keeping an oil-soaked cloth on the scalp, and holding a standing pool of oil on the head inside a fitted band (Shiro Basti). The classical method involves prior purification, careful fitting of the leather band and paste seal, a specific warm oil, and set holding times judged by the condition, with massage and oil removal afterward and a limited course length. These are clinical procedures needing proper set-up and judgement, so they are performed only by or under a trained Ayurvedic p","chapter":22},{"problem":"Eye drops and collyrium (Anjana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Depends on the condition (Vata, Pitta, Kapha or vitiated blood)","guidance":"This describes two external eye treatments from classical Ayurveda: medicated drops (Aschotana) and a medicated lid paste or powder (Anjana or collyrium). The texts stress careful choice of strength, temperature, dose and timing, and list many situations where the treatment should not be used at all. These are clinical eye-care procedures meant to be carried out only by a trained physician. They must never be tried at home, and they are not a substitute for seeing a qualified eye doctor. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should stop any prescribed medicine or delay proper medical care for an eye problem.","chapter":23},{"problem":"Eye nourishing (Tarpana)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Mainly Vata and Pitta; type chosen to suit the disorder","guidance":"Tarpana bathes the closed eye in warm medicated ghee or fat held within a paste wall around the eye socket, and Putapaka instills a concentrated roasted meat-and-herb juice in a similar way to restore eye strength. Both are specialist eye-nourishing procedures of classical Ayurveda that require precise preparation, timing and after-care and were carried out only by a trained physician. They must never be attempted at home and are not a substitute for modern eye care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should stop prescribed medicine or delay seeing a qualified eye doctor.","chapter":24},{"problem":"Blunt instruments","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"This chapter catalogues the blunt surgical instruments of classical Ayurveda, tools that grasp, hold, probe, lift, extract, suck and support rather than cut. They belong to a trained surgeon working in a proper clinical setting. Using any such instrument is a surgical act and must never be attempted at home. This information is educational only and is not a substitute for modern medical or surgical care. It makes no promise of a cure, and no one should delay proper medical treatment on the basis of it.","chapter":25},{"problem":"Sharp instruments","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"This chapter describes the sharp surgical instruments of classical Ayurveda and their uses, along with leeches and other accessory tools for drawing blood. All of it is surgery and bloodletting carried out by a trained surgeon in a proper clinical setting. None of it should ever be attempted at home. This is educational information only and is not a substitute for modern surgical or medical care. It promises no cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed treatment because of it.","chapter":26},{"problem":"Bloodletting (Siravyadha)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Mainly vitiated blood with Pitta and Kapha involvement","guidance":"Siravyadha is the controlled opening of a vein to draw off blood thought to be vitiated. It is a clinical surgical procedure of classical Ayurveda that required careful patient selection, correct technique, strict limits on how much blood was drawn, and skilled after-care, all in the hands of a trained physician. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern medical care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed medicine because of it.","chapter":27},{"problem":"Removing foreign bodies","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"—","guidance":"This chapter describes how classical Ayurvedic surgeons located and removed foreign bodies such as arrowheads and thorns, including which should and should not be removed and the many extraction techniques and instruments used. All of this is surgery carried out by a trained surgeon in a proper setting. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern emergency or surgical care. Nothing here promises a cure; anyone with an embedded foreign body or injury should seek qualified medical help without delay.","chapter":28},{"problem":"Surgical operations","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Varies with the swelling (Vata, Pitta, Kapha or vitiated blood)","guidance":"Shastrakarma is the surgical incising and draining of an abscess or swelling, with the careful pre-operative, operative and post-operative care, suturing and bandaging that classical Ayurveda describes. All of it is surgery carried out by a trained surgeon in a proper clinical setting, with correct timing and skilled wound care. It must never be attempted at home and is not a substitute for modern surgical or medical care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed treatment because of it.","chapter":29},{"problem":"Cautery (Agnikarma) and alkali (Kshara)","category":"Clinical Therapy","dosha":"Alkali avoided in aggravated Pitta and blood; strength chosen by dosha and severity","guidance":"Kshara (caustic alkali) and Agnikarma (thermal cautery or branding) deliberately destroy or seal tissue and are among the most powerful and hazardous procedures in classical Ayurveda. Both demand careful patient selection, precise application, correct strength and skilled after-care, and were used only by a trained physician or surgeon. They can cause serious harm and must never be attempted at home. This is educational information only and is not a substitute for modern medical or surgical care. Nothing here promises a cure, and no one should delay seeing a qualified doctor or stop prescribed medicine because of it.","chapter":30}]};
